    \includegraphics[width=0.4\linewidth]{fig/2-Datasets/L96_30.png}
    \caption{Lorenz–96 attractor projected onto the first onto the first three principal components using 40{,}000 data points.}
    \label{fig:dataset-L96}
\end{figure}




\subsection{Charney--DeVore model}

The Charney--DeVore (CdV) model is a simple six-variable system used to study large-scale atmospheric flow \cite{charney1979multiple}. It produces two regimes corresponding to blocked and zonal flow. In the classical view \cite{charney1979multiple}, these regimes are related to two fixed points where the model’s trajectory slows down. The fixed point for the blocked regime lies near the dense central region of the attractor, while the zonal regime is found near the back left corner \cite{strommen2023topological}. The blocked regime is more stable and quasi-stationary, while the zonal regime is more turbulent and chaotic \cite{pomeau1980intermittent}. In the phase space, the blocking state forms a dense, connected region, whereas the zonal state appears as many low-density loops spiraling outward across a wider area \cite{strommen2023topological}. These loops can be seen in raw data (see Fig.~\ref{fig:dataset-CDV}).

