\subsection{Results for the Charney--DeVore System}\label{subsec:cdv}

Recall that the CdV system has two regimes corresponding to blocking and zonal states. In phase space, the blocking regime forms a dense, connected region, while the zonal regime appears as several thin, low-density loops that extend over a wider area. 


The centrality method improves the detection of regime-related topological structures in the CdV system. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:CDV_comparison}, Gaussian KDE assigns low values to the thin loops, effectively removing them during the bifiltration process. This behavior is reflected in the KDE-based bifiltration diagram shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:CDV_result_KDE}. In \citet{strommen2023topological}, a direct-binning approach is used as an alternative to KDE. The direct-binning method better recovers fine-scale loop structures associated with the zonal regime. For example, as shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:CDV_Binning}, the direct-binning method identifies the first loop using about 40\

The centrality-based method, on the other hand, identifies regime-related features earlier in the filtration, and the main loops it detects have longer lifespans than those obtained using the binning method. A longer lifespan indicates that the detected feature is more persistent (with respect to distance parameter) and therefore more reliable. Fig.~\ref{fig:CDV_results} compares bifiltration diagrams from centrality-, KDE-, and binning-based methods. Fig.~\ref{fig:CDV_loops} illustrate the detection of the first persistent loops under these bifiltrations.


