\section*{Appendix A: Centrality Functions and the Distance-to-Measure}

Let $\mu$ be a positive (Borel) probability measure defined on a subset of the Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^d$, and let $m_0 > 0$ denote a fixed mass parameter smaller than the total mass of $\mu$. 
The \emph{distance function to the measure} (dtm) $\mu$ with parameter $m_0$ quantifies how far a point $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$ lies from the bulk of the mass distribution of $\mu$. 
It is defined as the average distance from $x$ to the smallest Euclidean balls centered at $x$ that contain a proportion $u \le m_0$ of the total measure. 
Formally, the dtm function is given by
\begin{equation}
d_{\mu, m_0, r}(x) = \left( \frac{1}{m_0} \int_{0}^{m_0} \delta_{\mu, u}(x)^r \, du \right)^{1/r},
\label{eq:dtm_measure}
\end{equation}
where
\begin{equation}
\delta_{\mu, u}(x) = \inf \{ t > 0 : \mu(B(x, t)) > u \}.
\label{eq:dtm_delta}
\end{equation}
Here, $\delta_{\mu, u}(x)$ represents the radius of the smallest Euclidean ball $B(x, t)$ centered at $x$ that contains a fraction $u$ of the total mass of $\mu$. 
This measure-theoretic formulation captures the geometric spread of $\mu$ around $x$ and provides a smooth, robust alternative to standard distance functions defined over discrete point sets.

For a finite sample $X = \{x_1, \ldots, x_n\}$ with uniform probability measure on it, the dtm reduces to its empirical form:
\begin{equation}
d_k(x) = \left( \frac{1}{k} \sum_{i=1}^{k} \|x - x_{(i)}\|^r \right)^{1/r},
\label{eq:dtm_empirical}
\end{equation}
where $k = \lfloor n m_0 \rfloor \in \mathbb{N}$ and $x_{(1)}, \ldots, x_{(k)}$ denote the $k$ nearest neighbors of $x$ under the Euclidean metric.

Finally, for consistency wih KDE output and interpretability, the dtm is normalized and inverted to define the \emph{local centrality function}
\begin{equation}
C_k(x) = 1 - \frac{d_k(x) - \min_j d_k(x_j)}{\max_j d_k(x_j) - \min_j d_k(x_j)},
\label{eq:centrality}
\end{equation}
so that higher $C_k(x)$ values correspond to points that are geometrically central within their local neighborhoods, while lower values indicate peripheral or transitional regions.


The integer $k$ controls the locality scale of the dtm:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Small $k$ emphasizes fine, local geometry and is sensitive to filamentary or loop-like features.
    \item Large $k$ smooths out noise and reflects more global geometric organization.
\end{itemize}
Since $d_{k_1}(x) \le d_{k_2}(x)$ for $k_1 < k_2$, increasing $k$ generates a continuous transition between local and global structure, analogous to a scale-space parameter.

In practical applications, $k/n$ acts as a normalized locality fraction; typical values lie in the range $10^{-4}$–$10^{-2}$ depending on sample size.

Here, we summarize key properties of the dtm (\cite{chazal2011geometric, chazal2018robust}):

The function $d_k(x)$ is positive and continuous on $\mathbb{R}^d$, as it is a mean of continuous nearest-neighbor distances. More importantly, it satisfies a \emph{1-Lipschitz} condition, which guarantees that small changes in $x$ lead to at most equally small changes in the value of $d_k(x)$. Formally, for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^d$,
\begin{equation}
| d_k(x) - d_k(y) | \le \| x - y \|.
\label{eq:lipschitz}
\end{equation}

This property is crucial in practice: it ensures that the dtm varies smoothly over the state space and (depending on the selected k) does not introduce artificial discontinuities even when the underlying data are irregularly sampled or contaminated by observational noise. In the context of atmospheric dynamics, this means that neighboring states in phase space—such as nearby realizations of large-scale circulation patterns—will produce similar centrality values, leading to stable and interpretable topological filtrations.


