We observe that, except for the cases in Tables~\ref{tab:example3}, \ref{tab:example8}, 
\ref{tab:example13}, and \ref{tab:example18}, the algorithm does not seem to converge to the global 
minimizer $\tau_{\mathrm{opt}}$. Especially, if $\alpha = 0$, then the iteration frequently stops 
in a stationary point, where the residual of the fixed point equation becomes comparatively small, 
but the limit substantially differs from $\tau_{\mathrm{opt}}$.
This underlines the non-convex nature of the switching-point-to-control map, which is the only source of non-convexity 
in this example. 
Note that the value of the objective is also significantly larger in stationary points different from $\tau_{\mathrm{opt}}$
compared to the examples in Table~\ref{tab:example3}, \ref{tab:example8}, 
\ref{tab:example13}, and \ref{tab:example18}, where $\tau_k \approx \tau_{\mathrm{opt}}$.
The situation slightly changes if the Tikhonov parameter is set to $\alpha = 10^{-6}$.
Here, we observe that the maximum number of 200 iterations is reached in all cases, 
whereas the iteration terminates in cases (i) and (ii), because the relative difference 
of two iterates becomes too small. The residuum in the fixed point equation is substantially smaller in cases (i) and (ii), too. 
This indicates that the maximum number of iterates is not sufficient in case $\alpha > 0$ and, with a larger number of 
iterations, the algorithm might be able to converge to the global minimizer with initial values different from 
the ones in Tables~\ref{tab:example13} and \ref{tab:example18}, too. 
The worse convergence behavior in case of positive $\alpha$ is surprising, since we have expected
a contrary behavior as the Tikhonov term usually stabilizes a numerical method.
This is subject to future investigations.

To summarize, we observe that we frequently do not converge to the global minimizer, 
even if the initial value is not that far from the optimal value $\tau_{\mathrm{opt}}$.
This underlines the non-convex nature of the problem under consideration.










