\section{Quantum Riemannian Cubics on $\CP^n$}

Although we have focused on the qubit case $n=1$, the pure-state space
for an $(n+1)$-level system is $\CP^n$ with the Fubini--Study metric.
We briefly outline the quantum Riemannian cubic with obstacle in this
setting.

\subsection{Fubini--Study metric and curvature}

Let $\mathcal{H}\cong\C^{n+1}$ and $S(\mathcal{H})$ be the unit sphere.
The projective space $\CP^n$ is the quotient $S(\mathcal{H})/U(1)$.
The Fubini--Study metric $g_{FS}$ can be described as follows: for
$[\psi]\in\CP^n$ with representative $\psi\in S(\mathcal{H})$ and tangent
vectors $X,Y\in T_{[\psi]}\CP^n$ represented by horizontal vectors
$\delta\psi_X,\delta\psi_Y\in T_\psi S(\mathcal{H})$ orthogonal to $i\psi$,
\begin{equation}
  g_{FS}(X,Y) =
  \Re\big(\langle \delta\psi_X,\delta\psi_Y\rangle\big),
\end{equation}
where $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle$ is the Hilbert-space inner product.

\begin{proposition}[Curvature of $\CP^n$]
The Fubini--Study metric has constant holomorphic sectional curvature.
In particular, $(\CP^n,g_{FS})$ is a compact Riemannian symmetric space.
The Riemann curvature tensor can be written in terms of $g_{FS}$ and the
complex structure $J$; see standard references on Kähler geometry.
\end{proposition}

This symmetric structure makes $\CP^n$ an especially natural stage for
quantum geometric variational problems.

\subsection{Quantum Riemannian cubic with obstacle on $\CP^n$}

Let $\gamma : [0,T]\to\CP^n$ be a smooth curve of pure states. The
quantum Riemannian cubic with obstacle avoidance on $\CP^n$ is defined as
a critical point of
\begin{equation}
  J(\gamma) = \int_0^T
  \left[
    \frac{1}{2}\|D_t\dot\gamma\|_{FS}^2 + V(\gamma)
  \right] dt,
\end{equation}
for a potential $V:\CP^n\to\R$ that encodes forbidden regions in state
space (for instance, states with excessive population on certain levels).

\begin{theorem}[Quantum cubic equation on $\CP^n$]
A smooth curve $\gamma : [0,T]\to\CP^n$ is a critical point of $J$ with
fixed $(\gamma(0),\dot\gamma(0))$ and $(\gamma(T),\dot\gamma(T))$ if and
only if it satisfies
\begin{equation}
  D_t^3\dot\gamma
  + R_{FS}\!\left(D_t\dot\gamma,\dot\gamma\right)\dot\gamma
  + \operatorname{grad}_{FS} V(\gamma) = 0,
\end{equation}
where $D_t$ is the Levi--Civita connection of $g_{FS}$, $R_{FS}$ its
curvature tensor, and $\operatorname{grad}_{FS} V$ its Riemannian gradient.
\end{theorem}

\begin{remark}
This is a direct analogue of Theorem~\ref{thm:ELgeneral}, now interpreted
in the quantum state manifold $(\CP^n,g_{FS})$. The Bloch-sphere case
corresponds to $n=1$, where the geometry reduces to that of $S^2$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Hamiltonian interpretation}

The unitary group $G=SU(n+1)$ acts transitively on $\CP^n$ and preserves
$g_{FS}$. A Hamiltonian $H(t)\in\mathfrak{su}(n+1)$ generates a unitary
evolution $U(t)$ and a projected quantum trajectory $\gamma(t)=[U(t)\psi_0]$.
The quantum Riemannian cubic problem on $\CP^n$ can thus be equivalently
viewed as a constrained variational problem on $SU(n+1)$ with a second-order
Lagrangian invariant under the isotropy subgroup of $[\psi_0]$, plus an
obstacle potential $V$ attached to certain submanifolds of $\CP^n$.

A full development of the corresponding Euler--Poincar\'e equations and
their relation to quantum splines in higher dimension is left for future
work.
\subsection{Hamiltonian Reconstruction for Quantum Cubics on $S^2$}

We now make explicit the relation between a quantum Riemannian cubic
$\vec n(t)$ on the Bloch sphere and Hamiltonian generators
$H(t)\in\mathfrak{su}(2)$.



Let $H(t) = \frac{1}{2}\vec h(t)\cdot\vec\sigma$ with $\vec h(t)\in\R^3$.
The associated Bloch equation is
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:BlochEq}
  \dot{\vec n} = \vec h(t)\times \vec n.
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Family of Hamiltonians generating a given $\vec n(t)$]
\label{prop:Hfamily}
Let $\vec n(t)\in S^2$ be a smooth curve with $\vec n\cdot\dot{\vec n}=0$.
Then any Hamiltonian of the form
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:Hfamily}
  \vec h(t) = \vec h_\perp(t) + \lambda(t)\,\vec n(t),
\end{equation}
with
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:perpComp}
  \vec h_\perp(t) = \vec n(t)\times \dot{\vec n}(t),
\end{equation}
and arbitrary scalar function $\lambda(t)\in\R$ reproduces the same
Bloch trajectory $\vec n(t)$ via \eqref{eq:BlochEq}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Since $\dot{\vec n}$ is tangent, we have $\vec n\cdot\dot{\vec n}=0$. For
any $\lambda(t)$,
\[
  \vec h(t)\times \vec n
  = \vec h_\perp(t)\times\vec n + \lambda(t)\,\vec n\times\vec n
  = \vec h_\perp(t)\times\vec n.
\]
Using the vector triple product,
\[
  \vec h_\perp\times\vec n
  = (\vec n\times\dot{\vec n})\times\vec n
  = (\vec n\cdot\vec n)\dot{\vec n} - (\vec n\cdot\dot{\vec n})\vec n
  = \dot{\vec n},
\]
which proves \eqref{eq:BlochEq}.
\end{proof}

Thus $\vec h_\perp$ is uniquely determined by the curve $\vec n(t)$,
whereas the parallel component $\lambda(t)\vec n(t)$ is physically
irrelevant at the level of the Bloch vector; it modifies dynamical
phases but not the state ray.


If $\vec n(t)$ is a quantum Riemannian cubic with obstacle avoidance,
then it satisfies \eqref{eq:explicitODE}. Differentiating the Bloch
equation \eqref{eq:BlochEq} repeatedly allows one to express
$\ddot{\vec n}$, $\dddot{\vec n}$ and $\ddddot{\vec n}$ in terms of
$\vec h$, $\dot{\vec h}$, $\ddot{\vec h}$, and $\vec n$. Substituting into
\eqref{eq:explicitODE} yields a fourth-order ODE for $\vec h(t)$ with
additional constraints from the norm of $\vec n$. Schematically, one obtains an equation of the form
\begin{equation}
\label{eq:HcubicSchematic}
  \mathcal{Q}(\vec h,\dot{\vec h},\ddot{\vec h},\dddot{\vec h};\vec n)
  + \nabla V(\vec n) = 0,
\end{equation}
where $\vec n$ and its derivatives are  determined by
\eqref{eq:BlochEq}.

\begin{remark}
In the absence of obstacles ($V\equiv 0$), the condition that $\vec n$
is a Riemannian cubic is equivalent to a cubic-like equation in $\vec h$
that is closely related to the quantum spline equation
$\dddot H + i[H,\ddot H]=0$ of \cite{BrodyHolmMeier2012}. The presence
of $\nabla V(\vec n)$ acts as a forcing term on this equation.
\end{remark}




