\subsection{Training Setup}\label{subsec:train_setup}

\paragraph{Model} We select the two model families, Qwen3 \citep{yang2025qwen3technicalreport} and Llama-3.2 \citep{dubey2024llama3herdmodels}. We use the pre-trained base checkpoints to prevent biased prior preference distributions from post-training. The projection head $W_p \in \mathbb{R}^{d_\mathrm{model}\times 2}$ is initialized with $\mathcal{N}(0, (d_\mathrm{model}+1)^{-1})$ \citep{huang2024the,hong2025on}.


\paragraph{Data} We use Skywork-Preferences-v0.2 \citep{liu_skywork-reward_2024}, a selected high-quality mixture of MagPie \citep{xu2025magpie}, WildGuard \citep{han2024wildguard}, OffsetBias \citep{park_offsetbias_2024}, and HelpSteer 2 \citep{wang2025helpsteerpreference}, covering diverse domains of human preference. We assume each dataset reflects a consistent implicit preference distribution, \eg WildGuard has a consistent preference bar for safety. For each training instance, we construct in-context demonstrations $\mathcal{C}=\{(x,y_w,y_l)\}_{j=1}^N$ with $N\in\{1,2,4,8,16\}$, sampled within train data. We use a prompt format without any arbitrary instructions in Appendix~\ref{apdx:template} to minimize template bias. The training details are listed in Appendix \ref{apdx:train_config}.



\subsection{Evaluation Setup}

We evaluate the test-time steerability of a \emph{single} ICRM by supplying domain-specific in-context demonstrations. To ensure that the in-context preference learning is properly assessed, both $\mathcal{C}$ and the evaluated preference pairs come from the \emph{same} underlying preference distribution: \eg both from WildGuard. Evaluating with four seeds, we randomly sample $N \in \{1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32\}$ preference pairs $(x, y_w, y_l)$ to construct $\mathcal{C}$. We then compare the scores for $(x', y'_w)$ and $(x', y'_l)$ conditioned on $\mathcal{C}$, where $(x', y'_w, y'_l)$ is drawn from the held-out data. We report the mean of four runs.


\paragraph{Single-objective test-time steerability} We employ three benchmarks: SafeRLHF \citep{ji2023beavertails}, HHH Alignment \citep{srivastava2023beyond}, and RM-Bench \citep{liu2025rmbench}. These sequentially cover (1) single-theme, (2) multi-theme, and (3) advanced or deterministic task-oriented preferences.

\paragraph{Multi-objective test-time steerability} We study ICRM's capacity to balance conflicting objectives via test-time steering. We select \textit{Safety-Should-Respond} and \textit{Safety-Should-Refuse} subsets of ``Safety'' domain of RM-Bench, comprising benign and adversarial prompts, respectively. We construct $\mathcal{C}$ by mixing demonstrations from these sources with a ratio $\alpha \in [0, 1]$, representing the proportion of helpfulness examples. By sweeping $\alpha$ across varying $N$, we map the Pareto frontier for responsiveness and refusal.

\paragraph{Baselines} We add three reward models with different modeling objectives: Bradley-Terry \citep{liu_skywork-reward_2024}, ArmoRM \citep{wang2024armo}, and GRM \citep{yang2024regularizing}.

