\subsection{Pareto Frontier Analysis}\label{subsec:pareto}
Figures \ref{subfig:moo_l32} and \ref{subfig:moo_q3} visualize the Pareto frontiers between responsive (``should respond'') and refusive (``should refuse'') behaviors on RM-Bench \citep{liu2025rmbench}. Each point corresponds to an evaluation conditioned on an in-context demonstration set $\mathcal{C}$ constructed by demonstrations with a fixed composition ratio $\alpha \in \{0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0\}$ for responsive behaviors and $1-\alpha$ for the other.

\paragraph{ICRM traces smooth Pareto frontiers via in-context preference composition} Across both model families in Figures \ref{subfig:moo_l32} and \ref{subfig:moo_q3}, ICRM produces continuous and monotonic trade-off curves, closely tracking the ideal frontier. In the multi-objective setting, the latent preference probability $z$ encodes a mixed preference over responsiveness and refusal behavior, as induced by the composition of in-context demonstrations $\mathcal{C}$. As $\alpha$ increases, the posterior mean $\mu = \mathbb{E}_{q\theta}[z]$ smoothly shifts toward higher ``Should Respond'' accuracy, while sacrificing ``Should Refuse'' accuracy in a controlled manner. This behavior demonstrates that the posterior mean does not collapse to a single objective but instead reflects the implicit preference distribution induced by $\mathcal{C}$, validating the multi-objective expressiveness of the Beta posterior parameterization.

\paragraph{ICRM with strong base model Pareto dominates static reward models} We overlay three static reward models on the same Pareto plane: Bradley-Terry \citep{liu_skywork-reward_2024}, URM \citep{lou2024urm}, and GRM \citep{yang2024regularizing}, all trained on the same preference data but optimized for fixed objectives. These baselines appear as isolated operating points, since they lack a mechanism for test-time trade-offs. With the Llama-3.2-3B base model, ICRM exhibits a wide range of steerability, spanning approximately $20\

In contrast, when paired with the stronger Qwen3-4B base model, ICRM attains Pareto frontiers that are competitive with or Pareto-dominant over two of the three static baselines across most operating regimes in Figure~\ref{subfig:moo_q3}. Comprehensively, these results indicate that while absolute Pareto dominance depends on backbone strength, ICRM uniquely provides smooth test-time steerability over multi-objective preferences, enabling flexible navigation of the trade-off space beyond what static reward models can offer.


\subsection{Hypervolume Analysis}
We quantify multi-objective performance using Hypervolume \citep[HV]{zitzler1999evolutionary}, a metric that measures the dominated volume of the Pareto frontier relative to a reference point, \ie $(0.0, 0.0)$. Since HV is defined over a set of non-dominated solutions, it is inherently applicable only to methods that induce a Pareto frontier; static reward models in Section \ref{subsec:pareto} correspond to single operating points and therefore do not admit a meaningful HV measurement.

\paragraph{Hypervolume increases with contextual evidence} Figure \ref{subfig:hv} shows that the HV of ICRM's Pareto frontiers increases monotonically with increasing $N$, from less than 0.95 to over 0.98. This indicates that additional demonstrations consistently expand the achievable trade-off region, rather than collapsing the reward toward a single objective.

\paragraph{Bayesian interpretation of hypervolume gains} This improvement in HV directly reflects the variational structure of ICRM. As $N$ increases, the posterior concentration factor $\tau$ grows, yielding sharper yet still steerable Beta posteriors. Beyond expanding the interior of the Pareto frontier, higher $N$ also increases the attainable width at the extremes ($\alpha=0$ and $\alpha=1$), which is shown at $N=32$ in both frontiers. This indicates that ICRM can realize more confident single-objective optima while retaining smooth interpolation between them. The resulting monotonic HV gains suggest that additional demonstrations contribute evidence rather than bias toward a fixed preference mode. Across all $N$, Qwen3-4B consistently achieves higher HV than Llama-3.2-3B, indicating a more expressive amortized posterior under the same test-time steering mechanism.



\section{ICRM in Reinforcement Learning}\label{subsec:rl}

Our variational construction naturally provides a principled extension of scoring in-context reward modeling. Given that the approximate posterior $q_\theta(z | x,y_w,y_l,\mathcal{C}) = \mathrm{Beta}(\alpha_q,\beta_q)$ is parameterized by \eqref{eq:variational_parameters} for a pair of responses, we can interpret $u_\theta$ as the local contribution to $\mu$ and $s_\theta$ as the local contribution to $\tau$ for a single $(x, y)$:
\begin{equation}
    R(x, y, \mathcal{C}) = \mathrm{Softplus}(s_\theta(x, y, \mathcal{C})) \times u_\theta(x, y, \mathcal{C}).
\end{equation}
Intuitively, $R(x, y, \mathcal{C})$ both addresses the \emph{directionality} of preference through $u_\theta$ and the \emph{strength of contextual evidence} through $s_\theta$, yielding a reward signal that is not only comparable across responses but also calibrated to the reliability of in-context demonstrations.

\subsection{Experimental Setup}

We evaluate ICRM in the reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) setting for mathematical reasoning by comparing it to a task-specific verifier. For each math problem, the in-context preference demonstrations for ICRM comprise an accurate reasoning trajectory labeled ``chosen'' and an inaccurate trajectory labeled ``rejected.'' We train Qwen2.5-1.5B-Base \citep{qwen2025qwen25technicalreport} on INTELLECT-MATH\footnote{\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/PrimeIntellect/INTELLECT-MATH-SFT-Data}} using GRPO \citep{shao2024deepseekmathpushinglimitsmathematical} under three reward configurations: (1) \textbf{ICRM}: Qwen3-4B-Base ICRM with $\lambda=0.1$ and $N=8$ demonstrations; (2) \textbf{Bradley-Terry (BT)}: Skywork-Reward-Llama-3.1-8B-v0.2 \citep{liu_skywork-reward_2024} trained on the same preference data; and (3) \textbf{Exact Match}: exact-match supervision against gold answers. The training details are provided in Appendix~\ref{apdx:rl_train_config}.

