\end{figure}

In Figure \ref{fig:mass_energy}, we compare the temporal evolution of the energy and the mass conservation error. As predicted by Theorem \ref{thm:main}, the conservation of mass holds up to machine accuracy, while the energy is dissipative. It is shown in Figure \ref{fig:mass_energy} that the energy of the CHNS model in the final stages is lower than that of the CH model, although there is a non-zero contribution of kinetic energy. Remarkably, the CHD system drives the phase separation much faster, such that even at $t = 1.3$ the process is significantly more advanced than in the other two models at $t=3$. It is important to recall that the velocity field is induced by the phase-field through the Korteweg stress.

