\subsubsection{Near Future}

For near future prediction the classical Good-Toulmin estimator (GTE),

$$\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(GT)}:= -\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}1_{N_s > 0}(-r)^{N_s},$$
has long been the dominant approach but little was known about it theoretically. We show that for any $r\in [0,1]$ and $t > 0$ the worst case average error of the GTE is within a factor of two from the worst case average error achievable by any eligible estimator. We show that no other linear estimator can achieve anything but the most trivial of performance gains on the GTE. We show that no other estimator which respects some natural problem symmetries other than precisely the GTE achieves a rate which is better than that of the estimator which always predicts zero, henceforth referred to as the null-estimator. We define a variance proxy and show that a central limit theorem holds with this variance proxy, settling the open problem \cite{contardi_2025_gaussian} of constructing principled prediction intervals. Practitioners \cite{adams_2022_victim} have previously estimated $\mu$ and then simulated the unseen species problem on $\hat{\mu}$ \cite{chao_2014_rarefaction} which lacks theoretical guarantees. 

In the generalized problem we explicitly compute the extra error incurred by moving from the classical to the generalized setting.
We show generalizations of all the minimax results to the generalized unseen species problem with sets of size bounded by $B$. We show that the extra error can be upper bounded not just proportionally to $B^2t$ (which gives the optimal rate) but in terms of the typically much smaller $B\IE[S_t]$. This means we get the expected rate not just in a uniform sense but also in $\IE[S_t]$, which is a much more delicate result.


\subsubsection{Intermediate Future}
In the intermediate future the variance of the GTE is so large as to make it unusable and one must seek other estimators. The most well-known alternative are the Smoothed Good-Toulmin estimators (SGTE) proposed by \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal}, which encompass the Efron-Thristed estimator of \cite{efron_1976_estimating}. They are linear estimators which work by averaging over stochastic truncations of the GTE. Specifically, if $L$ non-negative-integer-valued random variable then
$$\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(L)}=-\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}1_{N_s > 0}P(L \geq N_s)(-r)^{N_s},$$
is the SGTE (SGTE) associated to (the distribution of) $L$. In a suitably normalized metric such estimators achieve the rate $n^{-\frac{1}{r}}$ \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal}. There exists a constant $c$ so that in the same metric one cannot obtain a rate better than $n^{-\frac{c}{r}}$ \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal}. This means that SGTEs are minimax optimal "up to constants" \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal}, but because there is a constant in the exponent, their result still leaves room for meaningful improvement and we speak of their rates being pseudo-optimal.

We give a recipe, in terms of a certain optimization problem, for constructing linear estimators which are, up to an explicit multiplicative constant, optimal in the class of linear estimators (and consequently (at least) pseudo-optimal in general owing to the SGT being linear). Algorithmically searching for good estimators hearkens back to \cite{efron_1976_estimating}, but therein they were optimizing a-posteriori while we optimize a-priori. Unfortunately and somewhat mysteriously the proof of the optimality result does not give the rate. Our estimator is much better in the worst case and substantially better on several real data sets, especially when there are many rare species. We give a central limit theorem with a variance proxy for linear estimators but since the estimators are in general biased this does not immediately allow the construction of prediction intervals.

In the generalized problem with sets of bounded size we show quite generically that linear estimators do not suffer a rate degeneration compared to the classical problem. Consequently, the estimators of \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal} are psuedo-optimal also in this subset of the generalized problem, with the same rate gap as in the classical problem.

\subsubsection{Distant Future}
To make predictions beyond the impossibility results of \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal} some extra assumption is needed, perhaps the most natural one being a regular variation (power law tail) assumption. Namely, one assumes that for some constants $c >0$ and $\alpha \in (0,1)$,
\begin{align} \label{approx power law}
\nu(x) := \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}1_{\mu(\{s\}) > x} \approx cx^{-\alpha}.
\end{align}

The consequences of making such an assumption in the unseen species problem are briefly mentioned in \cite{benhamou_2017_concentration} and subsequently explored in detail in \cite{favaro_2023_nearoptimal}. From these works it is known that if $\mu$ satisfies (\ref{approx power law}) and one finds an accurate estimator $\hat{\alpha}$ for $\alpha$ then \begin{align}\label{induced estimator} 
    \hat{S}_{t,T} := S_t((1+r)^{\hat{\alpha}}-1),
\end{align} is an accurate estimator in the unseen species problem. The prediction problem is thus reduced to a parameter estimation problem. The authors of \cite{favaro_2023_nearoptimal} gave an estimator for $\alpha$, motivated by certain maximum likelihood considerations, which allowed the induced estimator for the generalized unseen species problem to achieve a rate of $\frac{\log(t)\log(r)^2}{t^\alpha}$ in a suitably normalized metric. They showed this rate to be optimal up to polylog factors. With $\phi_1$ being the number of species seen precisely once, we suggest using $\hat{\alpha}:= \frac{\phi_1}{S_t}$ instead, which was mentioned and shown to give rise to a multiplicatively consistent unseen species estimator in \cite{benhamou_2017_concentration}, which predates \cite{favaro_2023_nearoptimal}. We give a pre-asymptotic and explicit tail bound in terms of estimable quantities, allowing the construction of conservative prediction intervals. From it one also sees that the induced unseen species estimator does not suffer the $\log(t)$ factor in the rate. The rate improvement means that we improve the prediction horizon to $r(t)$ growing like $\exp\left(\sqrt{t^\alpha}\right)$ rather than $\exp\left(\sqrt{\frac{t^\alpha}{\log(t)}}\right)$. Experimentally we find that this somewhat marginal rate improvement translates to substantial improvements on real data. Moreover, despite it being non-linear, its simplicity allows us to show that it achieves the same rate on the generalized problem when the sets are of bounded size, implying that it achieves the minimax rate up to poly-log factors. The proof is via a new concentration inequality for, among other quantities, $S_t$ and $\phi_1$, for incidence data. It is obtained as an application of the work of \cite{bartroff_2018_bounded} on Stein's method for concentration via sized biased couplings for configuration models. Equivalently it gives concentration for the number of vertices in an edge exchangeable graph and for the number of vertices of a given degree. Such degrees were of interest in \cite{janson_2017_on}. On the Bayesian side it is known that if we one restricts oneself to $\mu$ satisfying (\ref{approx power law}) then the Pitman-Yor process serves as a reasonable prior and that Gaussian credible intervals can be constructed for this approach \cite{contardi_2025_gaussian}.  

\subsection{Structure} The following Section \ref{sec: symmetries} discusses some symmetries it would be desirable for the estimators to display.  Then the next three sections, \ref{sec: near}, \ref{sec: inter}, \ref{sec: distant} deal with the near future, intermediate future and distant future, respectively. These three sections are each split into two sub-sections, the first covers the classical problem and the second the generalized problem. Finally we compare the estimators empirically in Section \ref{sec: comparison}.

Full proofs are relegated to the appendix but when a proof permits an elucidating, short, high-level summary it is provided as a proof sketch.

\section{Linear Estimators and Problem Symmetries} \label{sec: symmetries} For a species $s\in\mathcal{S}$, recall that $N_s$ is the number of observations of $s$ in the sample and let $N_s'$ be the number of observations of $s$ we will make on the return expedition. For $i \in \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$, let $\phi_i := \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}1_{N_s = i}$ be the number of species observed exactly $i$ times and encode the sequence as a vector $\boldsymbol{\phi}:=(\phi_1,\phi_2,\ldots)$.

\begin{defn}
    We say that an estimator is \textbf{atemporal} if the estimator is a function only of $r$ and $\boldsymbol{\phi}$, so in particular does not (explicitly) depend on time. 
\end{defn}
Why atemporality is a natural property is most clear in the classical un-poissonized setting. There $n$, which is analogous to $t$, satisfies $n = \sum_{i=1}^\infty i\phi_i$. Thus allowing dependence on $n$ adds no extra expressive power. 
\begin{defn}
    We say that an estimator is \textbf{union-additive} if \begin{align*}
        \hat{S}_{t,T}(r,\boldsymbol{\phi})+\hat{S}_{\tilde{t},\tilde{T}}(r,\tilde{\boldsymbol{\phi}}) = \hat{S}_{t+\tilde{t},T+\tilde{T}}(r,\boldsymbol{\phi}+\tilde{\boldsymbol{\phi}}).
    \end{align*}
for all $\boldsymbol{\phi},\tilde{\boldsymbol{\phi}},t,\tilde{t}$.
\end{defn}
To motivate union-additvity, imagine that there are two biologists in locations with \emph{disjoint} sets of species collecting samples and making predictions for how many new species they will discover. Imagine also that there is a research group leader with access to the union of the data sets. Then the number of discoveries made by the group is the sum of the discoveries made by the researchers. Union-additivity asks that the predictions satisfy the same equality.

\begin{defn}
    We say that an estimator is $\boldsymbol{\phi}$-homogeneous if $\hat{S}_{t,T}(r,k\boldsymbol{\phi}) = k\hat{S}_{t,T}(r,\boldsymbol{\phi}).$
\end{defn}
To motivate $\phi$-homogeneity, imagine that we are sampling matching pairs of shoes. We can either see this as the classical unseen species problem with an individual being a pair of shoes or as the generalized unseen species problem with shoes as individuals and all sets of size two (one left and one right shoe, which we consider distinct). Then $\phi$-homogeneity asks that the prediction should not depend on which representation of the problem we choose.
\begin{defn}
    We say that an estimator is \textbf{linear} if there exists a function $H: \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0} \mapsto \mathbb{R}$, in general depending on $r$ and $t$ such that,
    \begin{align*}
    \hat{S}_{t,T} = \hat{S}^{(H)}_{t,T} := \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}H(N_s),
    \end{align*}
and $H(0)=0$.
\end{defn}
In our proofs we will also often tacitly assume that $H$ is such that linearity of expectation holds which is not restrictive. The condition $H(0)=0$ is enforced because unobserved species cannot contribute. Notice that $$\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}H(N_s) = \sum_{i=1}^\infty H(i)\phi_i,$$
so we could equally well have considered estimators which are linear in the $\phi_i$:s, justifying the name. We will often view $H$ as a sequence and so often write $H_i$ in place of $H(i)$. The main appeal of linear estimators is that they constitute a large enough class to contain performant estimators, yet is simple enough to reason about. However, the following proposition and remark say that they play well with the symmetries of the problem.
\begin{prop}
\label{prop:symmetries} If an estimator is atemporal then it is linear if and only if it is union-additive. In particular, if an estimator is atemporal and union-addititve then it is linear.
\end{prop}
\begin{rmk}
    Linear estimators are automatically $\phi$-homogeneous. However, $\phi$-homogeneous estimators need not be linear and our suggested estimator in the distant future regime is of this type.
\end{rmk}

\section{Near Future} \label{sec: near}
\subsection{Classical Setting}
\subsubsection{Minimax Optimality of the Good Toulmin Estimator}
Our feeling is that the following should be known but the literature seems not to contain the precise statement.
\begin{prop} \label{prop: GT uniform} For $t \geq 0,r \leq 1$,
\begin{align*}
        \IE[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}^{(GT)}_{t,T}|^2] &=\IE[S_{t,T}]+\IE\left[\sum_{s\in\mathcal{S}}1_{N_s>0}r^{2Ns}\right], \\
        \sup_\mu\IE[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}^{(GT)}_{t,T}|^2] &= r(r+1)t.
    \end{align*}
\end{prop}
Let $\mathcal{F}_t$ be the filtration generated by the Poisson processes.
\begin{thm} \label{thm: minimax general} For all $r \geq 0$ and $t \geq 0$ and all $\mathcal{F}_t$-measurable estimators $\hat{S}_{t,T}^*$ we have
$$\sup_{\mu}\IE[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}_{t,T}^*|^2] \geq rt.$$
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\renewcommand{\qedsymbol}{}
Computing the error in using the conditional expectation as an estimator.
\end{proof}
From this one sees that for $r \leq 1,$ the GT-estimator is minimax optimal to within a factor $1+r\leq 2$. Within the class of linear estimators one can find an even tighter lower bound, ruling out any non-trivial improvement on the GTE within this class.
\begin{thm}\label{thm: Linear Minimax} For any linear estimator $\hat{S}^{(H)}_{t,T}$ and any $t,r \geq 0$ we have $$\sup_{\mu} \IE[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}^{(H)}_{t,T}|^2] \geq r^2\left(\frac{t}{t+1}\right)^2+rt+\left(\frac{t}{t+1}\right)^2r^2t.$$
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\renewcommand{\qedsymbol}{}
We consider the error incurred on a distribution with a very large number of very rare species. This error is determined by $H_1$, which we then optimize over.
\end{proof}

If we restrict the class even more then we can show a substantial rate gap to any other estimator. We write $f(t) \lesssim g(t)$ to mean $f(t) \leq cg(t)$ for some constant $c$ independent of $t$ and all $t$ large enough.

\begin{thm}\label{thm: homogeneous linear minimax} Fix $r > 0$ and let $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H)}$ be an atemporal and union-additive estimator other than precisely the GTE, then
\begin{align*}
    \sup_{\mu} \IE[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}^{(H)}_{t,T}|^2] \gtrsim t^2.
\end{align*}
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\renewcommand{\qedsymbol}{}
The hypothesis on the estimator allows the bias to be written as a sum over the species with each term depending only on the product $p_st$. Now double $t$ and transform $\mu$ by, for every species creating a new species and transferring half the probability mass from the old species to the new species. For all the old species $p_st$ is unchanged since $p_s$ was divided by two and $t$ was multiplied by two. However, the sum now has had each term duplicated and hence is twice as large. From this we see that the bias can be made to grow linearly in time and hence the MSE grows quadratically. The GTE escapes by being unbiased.
\end{proof}
\begin{rmk}
The null-estimator achieves the same rate so either one achieves the optimal rate or the same rate achieved by the null-estimator. Since the GTE is dreadful for $r >1$ this also means that when $r>1$ there is no reasonable estimator which respects the symmetries of the problem. That is to say, in this sense prediction is impossible beyond $r=1$.
\end{rmk}

\subsubsection{Non-uniform Error Theory}

The following theorem allows the construction of principled prediction intervals. We estimate the variance by $$\hat{V}_t := \sum_s 1_{N_{s>0}}r^{2N_s}+\hat{S}_{t,T},$$
and let $\mathcal{N}(0,1)$ denote the standard normal distribution.
\begin{thm} \label{thm: GT Gaussianity}
If $r \leq 1$ and $\mathbb{V}[S_{t,T}-\hat{S}^{(GT)}_{t,T}] \rightarrow \infty$ as $t\to\infty$ then 
     $$\frac{S_{t,T}-\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(GT)}}{\sqrt{\hat{V}_t}} \rightarrow \mathcal{N}(0,1),$$
     in distribution as $t\to\infty$.
\end{thm}
If $\mathcal{S}$ is finite then there is not really an asymptotic regime to speak of and we have the following.
\begin{prop} \label{prop: Finite Support}
Let $\mu$ be supported on a finite set of species and fix $r < 1$. Then almost surely
\[
\begin{alignedat}{3}
S_{t,T} &\rightarrow 0, \qquad\qquad\qquad &
\hat{S}_{t,T} &\rightarrow 0, \qquad\qquad\qquad &
\hat{V}_t  &\rightarrow 0.
\end{alignedat}
\]
\end{prop}

\subsection{Generalized Setting}

Because the proof relies only on linearity of expectation the GTE remains unbiased in the generalized setting. It transpires that the error in applying it can be seen as the "usual" (classical) error $\delta(t) := \IE[S_{t,T}] + \IE[\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}1_{N_s > 0}r^{2N_s}]$ and an extra error term arising from the dependence between different species. We define some notation that will be useful for expressing this extra error and other quantities. For $x,y \in \mathcal{S}$ let
\begin{align*}
    M_{x \cap y} &:= \sum_{C \in 2^\mathcal{S}: C \supset \{x,y\} }\mu(C)
     &M_{x \cup y}:=  \sum_{C \in 2^\mathcal{S}: C \cap \{x,y\} \neq \emptyset }\mu(C) \\
    M_{x} &:= \sum_{C \in 2^\mathcal{S}: x \in C}\mu(C),
     &M_{x \backslash y}:= \sum_{C \in 2^\mathcal{S}: x \in C,y \notin C} \mu(C).
\end{align*}
We adopt the convention that $\sum_{x,y}$ means the sum over ordered pairs of distinct elements of $\mathcal{S}$. 
Let 
$$\epsilon(t):= \sum_{x,y}e^{-(1+r)M_{x \cup y}t}(e^{r(r+1)M_{x \cap y}t}-1).$$
\begin{thm} \label{thm: error decomposition}
In the generalized unseen species problem,
    $$\mathbb{E}[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}^{(GT)}_{t,T}|^2] = \delta(t)+\epsilon(t).$$
\end{thm} 
It may be that $\epsilon(t)$ is not small. To demonstrate this we give an example showing that the generalized unseen species problem is not always feasible. 
\begin{exam} \label{cave exam}
    Suppose all animals live in a single cave such that either none or all of them are discovered. This corresponds to a $\mu$ which assigns positive probability only to a single large set. By making this set arbitrarily large and tuning probability of discovery it is clear that we can make the prediction problem arbitrarily hopeless.
\end{exam}
We will therefore need extra assumptions. The one which will play a central role for us it the following.
\begin{defn}
    We say that $\mu$ is of \textbf{$B$-bounded arity} and write $\mu \prec B$ if $\mu(C) = 0$ for all $C \in 2^\mathcal{S}$ such that $|C| >B$. We say that $\mu$ is of \textbf{bounded arity} if it is of $B$-bounded arity for some $B \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 1}$.
\end{defn}
In practice $B$ may or may not be known. For example, the genome fragments consist of $B=100$ base pairs. Phone calls are between $B=2$ people. The card game Magic the Gathering (MTG) has packs of $B=15$ cards. On the other hand, when collecting butterflies $B$ is not known, in so far that it may be said to exist at all.
\begin{rmk}It is natural to ask whether one could get away with a weaker assumption than bounded arity. Perhaps the most natural would be to consider the mean size of the sampled sets. However, by tuning the probability of discovering the cave and the number of species it contains appropriately one sees that constructions like Example~\ref{cave exam} still cause issues and so the mean set size being small is not enough. See Remark \ref{rmk: weakening of bounded arity} for discussion of a weakening in the far-future regime.
\end{rmk}
Under bounded arity $\delta(t)$ may be controlled similarly to how it was before.
\begin{prop}\label{prop: mu-uniform bound generalized}
    If $\mu \prec B$ then,
    \begin{align*}
        \delta(t) \leq r^2 \IE[S_t] + \IE[S_{t,T}] \leq Br(r+1)t.
    \end{align*}
\end{prop}
\begin{rmk}\label{lifting}
It is straightforward to strengthen the lower bounds of the previous subsection to get the right dependence on $B$. For a given $\mu \prec 1$, form a $\mu \prec B$ by speciating each species into $B$ distinct variants without changing the probabilities of the sets. For example, if before the sets with non-zero probability were \{ant\}, \{bee\}, \{cat\} each with probability $\frac{1}{3}$ then make the possible outcomes \{ant-1,$\ldots$,ant-$B$\}, \{bee-1,$\ldots$,bee-$B$\}, \{cat-1,$\ldots$,cat-$B$\}, each with probability $\frac{1}{3}$. Reading over the proofs this can be seen to give us an extra constant $B^2$ in the lower bounds of the previous section.
\end{rmk}
Bounded arity is enough to get excellent control of $\epsilon(t)$.
\begin{thm} \label{eps constant*E}
    If $\mu \prec B$ then, $$\epsilon(t) \leq  r(r+1)(B-1)\IE[S_t].$$
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\renewcommand{\qedsymbol}{}
One imagines constructing $\mu$ by the following procedure. Start by giving zero mass to all sets. Order the sets in some fixed but arbitrary way. At the $n$:th step, slowly add mass to the $n$:th set. Computing the derivatives with respect to the added probability mass one sees that the left hand side grows slower than the right hand side and therefore ends up smaller once the construction is done.
\end{proof}
Combining Theorem \ref{thm: error decomposition}, Proposition \ref{prop: mu-uniform bound generalized}, Theorem \ref{eps constant*E}, Lemma \ref{lem: more linear bounds} and Remark \ref{lifting} one obtains the following.
\begin{cor} \label{cor: GT optimal rate generalized}For $r \leq 1$ the GTE is minimax-optimal to within a factor $r+1 \leq 2$ also for the generalized unseen species problem with $\mu$ of $B$-bounded arity for any $B$.
\end{cor}
However, this corollary undersells Theorem \ref{eps constant*E} which really establishes that $\epsilon(t)$ has the expected rate not just in $t$ but in $\IE[S_t]$.

It would be desirable to be able to estimate $\epsilon(t)$. We are not able to offer a genuinely satisfactory estimator, but the next three propositions give some suggestions. Let $N_{x \cup y}$ be the number of observations that include $x$ or $y$, $N_{x \cap y}$ the number of observations that include both $x$ and $y$, $N_{x \backslash y}$ the number of times $x$ has been observed without $y$ and $N_{x \otimes y}:= N_{x \backslash y}+ N_{y \backslash x}$.
\begin{prop}\label{prop: epsilon-estimator} Let $\hat{\epsilon}(t) := \sum_{x,y}(r^{2N_{x \cap y}}-(-r)^{N_{x \cap y}})(-r)^{N_{x \otimes y}}$. Then,
    $$\epsilon(t)=\mathbb{E}[\hat{\epsilon}(t)].$$
\end{prop}
Unfortunately some numerics suggest that $\hat{\epsilon}(t)$ is not particularly well-concentrated.

As expressed in the following proposition, if we assume a small multiplicative difference between the unions and the intersections we can take the number of pairs co-observed precisely once as a conservative estimator of $\epsilon(t)$.

\begin{prop}\label{Prop: epsilon-perfect-pairs}If $\left(1+\frac{r^2}{1+r}\right)M_{x \cap y} < M_{x \cup y}$ for all $x,y \in \mathcal{S}$ then $$\epsilon(t) \leq r(r+1)\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{x,y}1_{N_{x \cap y}=1}\right].$$
\end{prop}
\begin{prop}\label{prop: epsilon-connectedness} Let $A_{x,y}$ be the event that $x,y$ are co-discovered, namely the event that the first set which contains either $x$ or $y$ contains both. Then,
    $$\epsilon(t) \leq r(r+1)\sum_{x,y}\frac{M_{x \cap y}}{M_{x \cup y}} = r(r+1) \IE[\sum_{x,y}1_{A_{x,y}}].$$
\end{prop}
Note that $\sum_{x,y}1_{A_{x,y}}$ is not $\mathcal{F}_t$-measurable but that since likely most co-discoveries occur fairly early the number of co-discoveries up to time $t$ can serve as a reasonable approximation. Proposition \ref{prop: epsilon-connectedness} justifies the following heuristic: \emph{If the number of co-discovered pairs is small compared to the number of discovered species, then the dependence is negligible.}

\section{Intermediate Future} \label{sec: inter}
\subsection{Classical Setting}
\subsubsection{Algorithmically Constructing Linear Estimators}

Following previous authors we aim to construct linear estimators suited to $r>1$. These will not be a-temporal as to avoid being subject to Theorem \ref{thm: homogeneous linear minimax} via Proposition \ref{prop:symmetries}. Instead of trying to find an interpretable $H$ à la \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal} we aim to choose one algorithmically. At face value this is not a feasible optimization problem as one would need to optimize over both $H$ and $\mu$. However, we give the following theorem which alleviates the problem.
We denote the exponential generating function of $H$ (understood as a sequence) by
$$g_H(x):=\sum_{i=1}^{\infty}\frac{H_i}{i!}x^i,$$ and let $H^2$ denote the element-wise square of $H$.


\begin{thm}\label{thm: opt powerhouse} For $r,t > 0$ and a linear estimator $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H)}$ set
\begin{align*}
    G_H:=\left(\sup_{p \in [0,1]} \frac{e^{-pt}}{p}|1-e^{-rpt}-g_{H}(pt)|\right)^2
        &+ \sup_{q \in [0,1]} \frac{e^{-qt}}{q}(1-e^{-rqt}+g_{H^2}(qt)).
    \end{align*} Then,
\begin{align*}
    \frac{1}{82}G_H \leq \sup_\mu \IE[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}^{(H)}_{t,T}|^2] \leq G_H.
\end{align*}
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\renewcommand{\qedsymbol}{}
Imagine trying to construct a $\mu$ so that the bias is large. In species $s$ the bias is $b_s := \IE[1_{N_s =0}1_{N_s'>0}-H(N_s)] = e^{-p_st}(1-e^{-rp_st}-g_H(p_st))$. One can therefore interpret $\frac{e^{-p_st}}{p_s}|1-e^{-rp_st}-g_H(p_st)|$ as the "cost effectiveness" of "spending" $p_s$ to cause bias. Spending the total probability mass of $1$, the best cost-effectiveness upper bounds the maximal achievable bias. The variance is bounded in a similar way, giving the upper bound in the theorem. For the lower bound, if $p^*$ and $q^*$ (the points at which the suprema are attained) happen to be reciprocals of integers then one can either realize the bias bound or the variance bound by taking a uniform distribution on $\frac{1}{p^*}$ or $\frac{1}{q^*}$, symbols, respectively. Interpolating these gives a lower bound of $\frac{1}{2}G_H$. As they may not be reciprocals of integers significant additional care is needed.
\end{proof}
We do not believe that the lower bound is sharp. If one is willing to restrict oneself to "reasonable" $H$, which make a genuine attempt at the prediction problem, one can no doubt improve the lower bound substantially as such $H$ tend to have a uniformly bounded $b_s$ and small $q^*$ and $p^*$ (which are hence well-approximated by reciprocals of integers). The upper bound is sharp in that it is an equality for the GTE.

In light of Theorem \ref{thm: opt powerhouse} it is very natural to seek an $H$ that minimizes $G_H$. This turns out to be a well-behaved optimization problem. Critically, $G_H$ is convex and even strictly convex away from $q^*=0$ in the following sense.
\begin{prop}\label{prop: convexity} Let $\theta \in (0,1)$. For any sequences $H^{(1)},H^{(2)}$,
    \begin{align} \label{eq: convexity of GH}
        G_{\theta H^{(1)}+(1-\theta)H^{(2)}} \leq \theta G_{H^{(1)}}+(1-\theta)G_{H^{(2)}}.
    \end{align}
Moreover, if $H^{(1)} \neq H^{(2)}$ and $\theta$ are such that the $q^*$ associated to $\theta H^{(1)}+(1-\theta)H^{(2)}$ is non-zero and $G_{\theta H^{(1)}+(1-\theta)H^{(2)}} < \infty$, then strict inequality holds in (\ref{eq: convexity of GH}). 
\end{prop}
Convexity means we can hope to find a good $H$ by (sub-)gradient based methods. Moreover, the following theorem guarantees that there is something to converge to.
\begin{thm} \label{thm: existence and uniqueness}
The functional $G_H$ admits a minimizer. That is, for all $r,t>0$ there exists a $H^*$ such that $G_{H^*} \leq G_H$ for all other sequences $H$. Moreover there exists an $r_0$ such that for all $r > r_0$ there exists some $t_0(r)$ such that for all $t > t_0$ the minimizer is unique.
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\renewcommand{\qedsymbol}{}
The proof of existence is via the direct method in the calculus of variations. $G_H$ is a rather natural functional on the Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_t$ with inner product $\langle H^{(1)},H^{(2)} \rangle_t := \sum_{i=1}^\infty H^{(1)}_iH^{(2)}_i\frac{t^i}{i!}$ and so can be shown to be lower semi-continuous. This combines with convexity to give weak lower semicontinuity, the key requirement for the direct method.

For uniqueness one needs an argument that optimizers don't occur on the part of the space where strict convexity fails. The spirit of this argument is that $q^* = 0$ implies a bound on $g_{H^2}(qt)$, which forces the coefficients to be small enough that a large bias is unavoidable.
\end{proof}

\begin{rmk}
Our uniqueness proof relies on the fact that for $r$ large enough there exists an $x$ such that
$$rx-\sqrt{xe^{x}(r+r^2)-1+e^{-x}-r^2x}\sqrt{e^{x}-x-1} > 1-e^{-rx}.$$
Numerically this seems to be true down to $r_0 \approx 2.88$. For smaller $r$ different ideas would be needed, if uniqueness holds at all.
\end{rmk}
From Theorem \ref{thm: opt powerhouse} we obtain the following corollary. 
\begin{cor}\label{cor: optimality of H^*}Let $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H^*)}$ be the linear estimator associated to a (the) minimizer of $G_H$. Then for any $r,t > 0$ and any other linear estimator $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H)}$ we have 
\begin{align*}
    \sup_{\mu}\IE[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H^*)}|^2] \leq 82\sup_{\mu}\IE[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H)}|^2].
\end{align*}
\end{cor}
Since the GTE and SGTEs are linear, this in particular means that $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H^*)}$ achieves (at least) their rates and hence obtains the optimal rate for $r \leq 1$ and is minimax pseudo-optimal for $r > 1$.

\subsubsection{Implementation}
Of course on a computer we can only minimize over a finite dimensional space and so for the purposes of producing the figures in the present paper we restrict ourselves to $H$ such that $H_k=0$ for all $k$ larger than $10$. To minimize $G_H$ we approximate the suprema using softmax and use a gradient based method (L-BFGS-B) over a gradually refining grid. Code for the solver as well as the code used to generate all the figures in the paper, is available on our GitHub \footnote{github.com/EdwardEriksson/Unseen-Species}. We will sometimes abuse terminology/notation and speak of the finite-dimensional approximation as if it was $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H^*)}$.
\subsubsection{Comparing Worst Case Guarantees}

Once our solver finds an $H$ we evaluate $G_H$ on a very fine grid to get a bound on the mean squared error. In particular, one need not believe that our optimization procedure found the optimal $H$ to trust the bound on the MSE. It suffices that we can evaluate $G_H$ accurately for the found $H$. We also evaluate $G_H$ for the SGTE, hence obtaining a MSE guarantee for it. We show the worst case guarantees we can give by this method against those for the SGTE with the analysis of \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal} in Figure \ref{fig:UsVSOrlitskyRMSE}. 
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{LinearEstimatorComparison.png}
    \caption{Comparison of the worst case RMSE of the SGT (as guaranteed by the work of \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal}) and our estimator. For the SGT we choose the truncation distribution to be the binomial distribution with the suggested parameter choice of \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal}. We also evaluate $G_H$ for the SGT, obtaining MSE guarantees for the SGT from our analysis. Note that the top plots use log-log scales.}
    \label{fig:UsVSOrlitskyRMSE}
\end{figure}

It is clear that our worst case guarantees are much better and that most of the improvement is not from the analysis being better but rather from our choice of estimator. On the other hand, we find that  the suprema are attained by very small $p^*,q^*$, which suggests that distributions with many rare species are binding on the optimization problem. This is natural as we are considering an absolute rather than a relative error and $\mu$ with few rare species are "easy" in this metric. This means that we should not expect much if any improvement on the SGTE for $\mu$ which have few rare species. We find that this is true empirically. See the uniform distribution and MTG parts of Figure \ref{fig:Key} and surrounding discussion. Relatedly, we note that Theorem \ref{thm: opt powerhouse} can easily be adapted to the assumption of lower bounded probabilities which is a common assumption in support size estimation \cite{wu_2019_chebyshev}. Specifically, if one restricts the supremum to be over $\mu$ with no species less probable than $p_0$ for some $p_0>0$, then the upper bound in Theorem \ref{thm: opt powerhouse} holds with the suprema restricted to $[p_0,1]$. Thus one can construct, using essentially the same methodology, estimators which are more well-suited to the regime of few rare species.

\subsubsection{Central Limit Theorem for Linear Estimators}

For a linear estimator we suggest estimating the variance by
$$\hat{V}^{(H)}_t := \hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H)}+\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}H(N_s)^2.$$
With this variance proxy we give a fairly generic CLT for linear estimators.
\begin{thm} \label{thm: CLT Intermediate}
Let $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H)}$ be a linear estimator, not necessarily atemporal, and let $r(t)$ possibly vary in time. Suppose that $\IE[\hat{S}^{(H)}_{t,T}] \geq 0$ and $|b_s| \leq c$ uniformly in time for some constant $c$. Suppose also that

\begin{align*}
    \frac{|\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}{b_s}|+\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}b_s^2+||H||_\infty^2+1}{\mathbb{V}[S_{t,T}-\hat{S}_{t,T}]} \rightarrow 0.
\end{align*}
Then,
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{S_{t,T}-\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H)}-\mathbb{E}[S_{t,T}-\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H)}]}{\sqrt{\hat{V}_t}} \rightarrow \mathcal{N}(0,1).
    \end{align*}
\end{thm}
\begin{rmk}
Theorem \ref{thm: CLT Intermediate} can be seen to imply Theorem \ref{thm: GT Gaussianity} by noticing that the GTE has $b_s \equiv 0$ and that for $r\leq 1$, $||H||_\infty \leq 1$.
\end{rmk}
\begin{rmk}
The authors of \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal} give upper bounds on $b_s$ and $||H||_\infty$ for their estimators, so for suitable $\mu$, $r(t),$ they will satisfy the assumptions of the theorem.
\end{rmk}
\subsection{Generalized Setting}

Let $R_t = \sum_{x,y}1_{N_{x \cap y}=0}1_{N_{x \cap y}'>0}$ be the number of pairs which have not been co-observed but which are about to be and let $Q_t = \sum_{x,y}1_{N_{x \cap y}>0}$ be the number of pairs that have been co-observed.
\begin{thm}\label{thm: linear generalization pairs abc}
Let $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H)}$ be a linear estimator with $H$ chosen such that, with $N,N'$, independent Poisson random variables with means $\lambda,r\lambda$ there exists a function $f$ such that $\IE[H(N)-1_{N=0}1_{N'>0}] \leq f(\lambda,r)$ and $f$ is non-decreasing in $\lambda$. Then,
\begin{align*} 
    \IE[|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}_{t,T}|^2] &\leq \IE[S_t]+||H||_{\infty}^2\IE[S_{t,T}]+(\sum_{s}f(M_st,r))^2-\sum_{s}f(M_st,r) \\
    &+\IE[R_t](||H||_\infty+1)^2+\IE[Q_t]||H||_\infty^2.
\end{align*}
\end{thm}
We also provide the following estimates.
\begin{lem} \label{lem: more linear bounds}If $\mu \prec B$ then,
\[
\begin{alignedat}{4}
\IE[S_t] &\leq Bt, \hspace{1.0em} &
\IE[S_{t,T}] &\leq Brt, \hspace{1.0em} &
\IE[R_t] &\leq rB(B-1)t, \hspace{1.0em} &
\IE[Q_t] &\leq B(B-1)t. \hspace{1.0em} &
\end{alignedat}
\]
\end{lem}
The combination of Theorem \ref{thm: linear generalization pairs abc} and Lemma \ref{lem: more linear bounds} means we can bound the error in using linear estimators on incidence data in quite some generality. In particular, since \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal} shows that for SGTEs, $||H||_\infty \leq \IE[r^L]$ and $\IE[1_{N_s=0}1_{N_s'>0}-H(N_s)] \leq (1-e^{-rM_st})\xi_L(r)$ (see their paper for the definition of $\xi$) which is increasing in $M_st$ and $\sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}} (1-e^{-rM_st})\xi_L(r) = \IE[S_T]\xi_L(r)$ we have the following corollary.
\begin{cor} \label{Orlitsky generalization}
    The SGTEs of \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal} including the Efron-Thristed estimator, achieve the same rate on the generalized unseen species problem with $\mu \prec B$ for any $B$ as they do in the classical unseen species problem. Since allowing $\mu \prec B > 1$ only makes the problem harder this means that the SGTEs are pseudo-optimal in the bounded arity version of the generalized problem with the same rate gap as in the classical problem. 
\end{cor}

\section{Distant Future} \label{sec: distant}


\subsection{Classical Setting}Recall that in the distant future regime the unseen species problem essentially reduces to the problem of estimating $\alpha$ and that this may be done in different ways. We give a CLT-like result for an unseen species estimator obtained in this way. We write $f(x) \sim g(x)$ to denote $\lim_{x \rightarrow 0}\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = 1$.

\begin{thm} \label{thm: CLT Distant} Let $\nu(x) \sim cx^{-\alpha}$ for some constants $c > 0$ and $\alpha \in (0,1)$ and let $r \in \mathbb{R}_{>0}$ be fixed. Let $\hat{\alpha}$ be any estimator of $\alpha$ which converges to $\alpha$ in probability. Let $\hat{S}_{t,T} := S_t((1+r)^{\hat{\alpha}}-1)$ be the induced estimator for $S_{t,T}$. Let $\sigma^2 := \Gamma(1-\alpha)[(2^\alpha-1)(1+r)^\alpha-2(1+r)^\alpha((1+\frac{1}{1+r})^\alpha-1)+2^\alpha-1]$. Then,
    \begin{align*}
        \frac{\hat{S}_{t,T}-S_{t,T}-\IE[S_t](1+r)^{\hat{\alpha}}+\IE[S_T]}{t^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}} \rightarrow \mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^2).
    \end{align*}
Moreover, if $\hat{\alpha}$ estimates $\alpha$ at a better rate than $\frac{1}{\log(t)}$ then the statement continues to hold upon replacing the $\alpha$ in the denominator by $\hat{\alpha}$. Note also that by the continuous mapping theorem $\hat{\sigma} \rightarrow \sigma$ where $\hat{\sigma}$ is the plug-in estimator using $\hat{\alpha}$.
\end{thm}
 We also give the following straightforward proposition, aimed at giving a quantitative idea of the size of the bias-like term in the above.
\begin{prop}\label{lem: alpha-clt-rate-maker} If $|\nu(x)-cx^{-\alpha}| \leq f(x)$ for some function $f$ for all $x \in (0,1)$ and some constants $c > 0,\alpha \in (0,1)$ then, almost surely,
\begin{align*}
    |\IE[S_t](1+r)^{\hat{\alpha}}-\IE[S_T]| &\leq c(1+r)\log(1+r)\Gamma(1-\alpha)t^\alpha|\hat{\alpha}-\alpha|, \\
    &+ (1+r)t\mathcal{L}[f](t)+T\mathcal{L}[f](T), 
\end{align*} where $\mathcal{L}[f](t)$ denotes the Laplace-transform of $f$. 
\end{prop}

\subsection{Generalized Setting}
\label{subsec: Distant Future, Generalized Setting}
We generalize $\nu(x)$ in the natural way, namely by setting $\nu(x) := \sum_{s \in \mathcal{S}}1_{M_s > x}.$
\begin{thm}\label{thm: alpha-rate} Let $\hat{\alpha} = \frac{\phi_1}{S_t}$. Fix $\epsilon > 0$. If there exist constants $c,\alpha,K$ such that $|\nu(x)-cx^{-\alpha}| \leq Kx^{-\frac{\alpha}{2}}$ for all $x \in (0,1)$ then there exists a constant $C(\epsilon,c,\alpha,K)$ so that
\begin{align*}
    \limsup_{t \rightarrow \infty}P\left(|\alpha-\hat{\alpha}| > \frac{C}{t^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}}\right) < \epsilon.
\end{align*}
\end{thm}
This is a factor $\sqrt{\log(t)}$ improvement on Theorem 2 of \cite{favaro_2023_nearoptimal}. We assumed a slightly better power law approximation, but if we make only the weaker assumption we recover their rate, so our result is strictly better. We note that this seemingly marginal rate improvement makes the difference between the bound on the bias-like term in Proposition \ref{lem: alpha-clt-rate-maker}, after the division by $t^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}$, staying bounded or going to infinity.

\begin{rmk}Because our suggested estimator for alpha is invariant under the map $\boldsymbol{\phi} \rightarrow k\boldsymbol{\phi}$ the induced estimator $\hat{S}_{t,T}$ is $\phi$-homogeneous while that of \cite{favaro_2023_nearoptimal} is not. 
\end{rmk}

Let $S_t^{(i)} = \sum_{j=i}^\infty \phi_j$ be the number of species seen at least $i$ times and note that $S_t=S_t^{(1)}$. We give the following concentration inequality.
\begin{lem}\label{lem: new concentration} Let $\mu \prec B$. Then,
    \begin{align*}
        P(S_t^{(i)}-E[S_t^{(i)}] \geq -z) &\leq \exp\left(-\frac{z^2}{2((B-1)i+1)E[S_t^{(i)}]}\right), \\
        P(S_t^{(i)}-E[S_t^{(i)}] \geq z) &\leq \exp\left(-\frac{z^2}{2((B-1)i+1)\IE[S_t^{(i)}]+\frac{2}{3}((B-1)i+1)z}\right).
    \end{align*}
\end{lem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\renewcommand{\qedsymbol}{}
An application of the work of \cite{bartroff_2018_bounded} on concentration via size-biased couplings for configuration models. A bounded size-biased coupling can be constructed since $\mu$ is of bounded arity, which implies concentration.
\end{proof}
\begin{rmk} Note that Lemma \ref{lem: new concentration} does not require a power law approximation to hold.
\end{rmk}
\begin{cor}\label{cor: phi concentration}
Lemma \ref{lem: new concentration} implies that for $\phi_i = S_t^{(i)}-S_t^{(i+1)}$ we have,
\begin{align*}
    P(|\phi_i-\IE[\phi_i]|>z) &\leq \exp\left(-\frac{z^2}{8((B-1)i+1)E[S_t^{(i)}]}\right) \\
    &+ \exp\left(-\frac{z^2}{8((B-1)i+1)\IE[S_t^{(i)}]+\frac{4}{3}((B-1)i+1)z}\right) \\
    &+\exp\left(-\frac{z^2}{8(Bi+B-i)E[S_t^{(i+1)}]}\right)\\
    &+ \exp\left(-\frac{z^2}{8(Bi+B-i)\IE[S_t^{(i+1)}]+\frac{4}{3}(Bi+B-i)z}\right).
\end{align*}
\end{cor}
\begin{rmk}\label{rmk: weakening of bounded arity} As described on page 3285 of \cite{bartroff_2018_bounded}, the authors of \cite{cook_2018_size} show that the bounded arity assumption in Lemma \ref{lem: new concentration} can be relaxed to $\mu$ being such that there exists a size-biased coupling of $S_t$, which we denote by $S_t^{(b)}$, so that there exists a $p \in (0,1]$ such that for all $x \in \mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}$,
\begin{align*}
    P(S_t^{(b)}-S_t \leq B|  S_t^{(b)} \geq x) &\geq p,\\
    P(S_t^{(b)}-S_t \leq B| S_t^{(b)} \leq x) &\geq p.
\end{align*}
As previously mentioned, $B$-bounded arity allows the construction of a size-biased coupling so that $|S_t^{(b)}-S_t| \leq B$ almost surely.
\end{rmk}
\begin{rmk} In the case $B=1$, the variance factors in Lemma \ref{lem: new concentration} are never better than Proposition 3.4 of \cite{benhamou_2017_concentration} and sometimes worse. For instance, for $i=1$ our result has variance factor $\IE[S_t]$ while theirs has $\IE[\phi_1]$.
\end{rmk}
The following is the main theorem of the section.
\begin{thm} \label{thm: far future main}
    Let $\hat{S}_{t,T}:=S_t((1+r)^\frac{\phi_1}{S_t}-1)$, $p \in (0,1)$ and $q:= 1-p$. Suppose that $\mu$ is such that for some constants $c>0$ and $\alpha \in (0,1)$ we have $|\nu(x)-cx^{-\alpha}| \leq f(x)$ for all $x \in (0,1)$. Let
\begin{align*}
    d(z) &:= \frac{pz c\Gamma(1-\alpha)t^\alpha(1+r)^{-\alpha}}{(1+2\ln(1+r))c\Gamma(1-\alpha)t^\alpha+pz(2-\alpha)\ln(1+r)(1+r)^{-\alpha}}.
\end{align*}
Then for $z < \frac{1}{p}(1+r)^\alpha (1+2\log(1+r))c\Gamma(1-\alpha)t^\alpha$,
\begin{align*}
    P(|\hat{S}_{t,T}-S_{t,T}|>z) &\leq \exp \left(-\frac{(qz-T\mathcal{L}[f](T))^2}{2B\IE[S_T]}\right)\\
    &+ \exp\left(-\frac{(qz-T\mathcal{L}[f](T))^2}{2B\IE[S_T]+\frac{2}{3}B(qz-T\mathcal{L}[f](T))}\right)\\
    &+\exp\left(-\frac{(d(z)-t\mathcal{L}[f](t))^2}{2B\IE[S_t]}\right) \\
    &+\exp\left(-\frac{(d(z)-t\mathcal{L}[f](t))^2}{2B\IE[S_t]+\frac{2}{3}B(d(z)-t\mathcal{L}[f](t))}\right) \\
    &+\exp\left(-\frac{(d(z)+t^2\mathcal{L}[f]'(t))^2}{(4B-2)\IE[S_t^{(2)}]}\right) \\
    &+\exp\left(-\frac{(d(z)+t^2\mathcal{L}[f]'(t))^2}{(4B-2)(\IE[S_t^{(2)}])+\frac{4B-2}{3}(d(z)+t^2\mathcal{L}[f]'(t))}\right).
\end{align*}
\end{thm}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
\renewcommand{\qedsymbol}{}
The application of Lemma \ref{lem: new concentration} along with a lemma giving bounds for a certain transcendental equation arising from the sensitivity to deviations in the random variables.
\end{proof}
The lemma was envisioned in conversation with Anthropic's Claude and formalized in LEAN by Harmonic's Aristotle.

Define the normalized error metric $\ell_{\alpha}(S_{t,T},\hat{S}_{t,T}) := \frac{|S_{t,T}-\hat{S}_{t,T}|^2}{(rt)^{2\alpha}}.$
\begin{cor}\label{cor: far future rate} If $r(t)$ is such that $\frac{r(t)}{\exp{(\sqrt{t^\alpha})}} \rightarrow 0$, $f(x) \leq Kx^{\frac{\alpha}{2}}$ for all $x \in (0,1)$ and $\hat{S}_{t,T} := S_t((1+r)^{\phi_1/S_t}-1)$ then for every $\epsilon > 0$ there exists a constant $C(\epsilon,\alpha,c,K,p)$ such that
\begin{align*}
    \limsup_{t \rightarrow \infty}P(\ell_{\alpha}(S_{t,T},\hat{S}_{t,T}) > \frac{C\log(r)^2}{t^\alpha}) < \epsilon.
\end{align*}
\end{cor}
\begin{rmk} \label{rmk: strong rate}
    We thank Alexander Fuchs-Kreiss for adapting the techniques of \cite{favaro_2023_nearoptimal} to give a purely asymptotic argument that the above can in fact be strengthened to $r(t) \lesssim \exp(t^{\frac{\alpha}{2}})$ and also for many valuable conversations regarding the present work. This is a factor $\log(t)$ improvement on Theorem 3 of \cite{favaro_2023_nearoptimal} but under a slightly stronger assumption on the power-law approximation. However, if we make only the weaker assumption we end up with their rate, so our result is strictly better.
\end{rmk}

\section{Experiments} \label{sec: comparison}

\subsection{Task}
For $r > 1$ we compare the performance of a variety of estimators. Our prescribed task is modeled after the use case in \cite{adams_2022_victim}. Following \cite{efron_1976_estimating},  \cite{wu_2019_chebyshev} and \cite{orlitsky_2016_optimal} we consider a person reading Hamlet. They know the total number of words in the play and aim to predict the total number of distinct words from reading an initial portion of the piece. Setting $\hat{S}_T := S_t + \hat{S}_{t,T}$ we see that this is an instantiation of the unseen species problem. We study absolute relative error, namely $\frac{|\hat{S}_T-S_T|}{S_T}$. When it makes sense to do so we give both the performance when the data is in the true temporal order and an average over $100$ shuffles to reduce noise. The shuffling is justified in so far that we believe in exchangeability. We prefer this to sampling i.i.d. from the empirical distribution as that underestimates the number of rare species and artificially introduces exchangeability.

\subsection{Data}

In addition to Hamlet we consider data sets of butterfly collecting \cite{yu_2025_spatial}, messages on a social media network \cite{panzarasa_2009_patterns} (sub-sampled), genome coverage (SRA code SRX151616) (restricted to the first $10^5$ genome locations, sub-sampled), simulated packs of MTG cards \cite{joshbirnholz_2025_github} and some synthetic data ($N=2000$ samples) from the uniform distribution over either $M=10^3$ or $M=10^4$ species. For all but the Hamlet data we use the generalized unseen species model. The data is described in more detail in Appendix 2.

\subsection{Benchmarks}
We compare $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H^*)}$, (\emph{$H^*$}) and our suggested distant future estimator (\emph{Ratio-$\alpha$}) to several competitors. A naive benchmark is obtained from the null estimator (\emph{Trivial}). We include the binomial smoothing SGTE with the suggested parameters (\emph{SGT}) and the power law estimator of \cite{favaro_2023_nearoptimal} (\emph{MLE-$\alpha$}). We also compare against the method of \cite{daley_2014_modeling} (\emph{Pade-GT$[2,3]$}) developed for applications in genome coverage. We also implement the support size estimator of \cite{wu_2019_chebyshev} (\emph{Chebyshev}). In their notation we set $k$ to be the corpus length and $c_0=0.45,c_1 = 0.5$ as their experimentally chosen values.
\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1.0\linewidth]{UnseenKeyFigure.png}
    \caption{Empirical Comparison of Performance of Estimators}
    \label{fig:Key}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Results}

See the result in Figure \ref{fig:Key}. We see that for the messages data the plots for the true order and the permutation average look totally different. This strongly suggests that exchangeability was severely violated and that the model is therefore not a good fit. One sees that the excellent performance of our estimators on the permutation average does not translate into good predictions under the temporal ordering. This serves as an important reminder that exchangeability is a non-trivial modelling assumption and that applications may require specialized models.

We note that since the unseen species problem resembles support estimation only for large $r$ it is natural to expect the Chebyshev-polynomial-based estimator of \cite{wu_2019_chebyshev} to perform poorly for small $r$, possibly explaining the U-shaped error curve. It appears that the Padé-based estimator is not competitive with the other estimators.

We find that our distant future estimator fairly consistently outperforms that of \cite{favaro_2023_nearoptimal} and that in as previously discussed, the worst-case improvements of $\hat{S}_{t,T}^{(H^*)}$ over the SGT translate to improvements when new discoveries are common, but not when they are rare. Neither the linear nor the power law estimators are consistently better than the others across data sets. No doubt in part due to variations in how good the power law approximation is.

