\begin{center}
\textbf{\large Abstract}
\end{center}
We propose a metric-dependent geometric variant of factorization homology in conformally flat Riemannian geometry for $d \geq 2$.
We introduce a symmetric monoidal category of germs of d-dimensional Riemannian manifolds and orientation-preserving conformal open embeddings, and its full monoidal subcategory generated by flat disks. 
A conformally flat $d$-disk algebra is a symmetric monoidal functor from this disk category to a target category; in this paper we take the target to be $\mathrm{IndHilb}$, the ind-category of Hilbert spaces, which provides a mathematical formulation of $d$-dimensional conformal field theories.


The (1-categorical) left Kan extension of an $\mathrm{IndHilb}$-valued conformally flat $d$-disk algebra defines a metric-dependent invariant of conformally flat manifolds. Under suitable positivity and continuity assumptions, we prove that its value on the standard sphere $(S^d,g_{\mathrm{std}})$ reproduces the sphere partition function of the associated conformal field theory. For $d>2$, we construct nontrivial $\mathrm{IndHilb}$-valued conformally flat $d$-disk algebras from unitary representations of $\mathrm{SO}^+(d,1)$.

\tableofcontents





\begin{center}
\textbf{\large Introduction}
\end{center}

Factorization homology (also known as topological chiral homology) is a homology-type theory
introduced by Lurie and further developed in subsequent work of Ayala--Francis \cite{Lurie2, AF1}.
Taking a $d$-disk algebra $A$ as input data, factorization homology assigns invariants of
$d$-dimensional manifolds via the left Kan extension of $A$:
\begin{equation}
\label{eq_intro_left}
\begin{tikzcd}
\mathrm{Disk}_d
  \arrow[r,"A"]
  \arrow[d,hook,"i"']
&
\mathcal{C}
\\
\mathrm{Mfld}_d
  \arrow[ru,dashed,"\mathrm{Lan}_{A}"']
&
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Factorization homology is formulated in an $\infty$-categorical setting, and its output
is closely related to metric-independent field theories, i.e., \emph{topological field
theories} \cite{Lurie3, Sc, CS, AFR, AFT, AF2}.

In this paper, we propose a metric-dependent geometric variant of factorization
homology in conformal Riemannian geometry for $d \geq 2$.
We introduce the category $\Embc$ of germs of $d$-dimensional Riemannian manifolds and
orientation-preserving conformal open embeddings, and its full monoidal subcategory $\Disk$ generated by the flat unit disk.
A symmetric monoidal functor from $\Disk$ to a target category $\cC$ provides
an analogue of a $d$-disk algebra in conformally flat geometry and gives a
mathematical formulation of $d$-dimensional \emph{conformal field theories} (CFTs), ``metric-dependent'' quantum
field theories with conformal symmetry.
We call such a functor a \emph{conformally flat $d$-disk algebra}.
While the choice of the target category $\cC$ is a fundamental issue in the
formulation of quantum field theory, in this paper we take $\cC$ to be the ind
category $\Ind \Hilb$ of the category of separable
Hilbert spaces $\Hilb$.













In this paper, we prove the following:
\begin{enumerate}
\item
For a conformally flat $d$-disk algebra $A$, the (1-categorical) left Kan extension along $\Disk\hookrightarrow \Embc$ defines a symmetric monoidal functor
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{Lan}_A:\Embc \rightarrow \Ind\Hilb,
\end{align*}
which yields metric-dependent invariants of conformally flat Riemannian manifolds (Theorem \ref{prop_ind_CF}).
\item
Under suitable positivity and continuity assumptions on $A$, the value on the standard sphere,
$\mathrm{Lan}_A(S^d,g_\std)$, reproduces the sphere partition function of the associated CFT (Theorem~\ref{cor_left_nonzero}).





\item
For $d \geq 3$, there exist explicit $\Ind \Hilb$-valued conformally flat $d$-disk algebras (Theorem \ref{thm_CF}). These algebras are ind-objects of unitary representations of $\SO^+(d,1)$ on Hilbert spaces and correspond to the CFT of the massless free scalar field.
\end{enumerate}

Quantum field theory (QFT) has both a geometric aspect, described by factorization
algebras developed by Beilinson--Drinfeld and Costello--Gwilliam, and a
real-analytic aspect, described by Hilbert spaces and unitary
representations by G\r{a}rding-Wightman \cite{BD,CG1,CG2,GW}.
In the later setting, quantum fields are typically \emph{unbounded operators} on Hilbert spaces, which makes them difficult to handle categorically. 
For this reason, it has not been clear whether factorization-algebra formalism can be placed in an operator-algebraic setting.

Our key observation is that if one defines the category $\Embc$ naively, then unbounded operators inevitably appear in the construction of examples, as one would a priori expect from G\r{a}rding-Wightman axioms.
Remarkably, for $d \geq 3$ there is a geometric criterion under which these operators become genuinely bounded: a necessary and sufficient condition is given in Theorem~\ref{thm_unbounded}. 
This motivates our framework combining the ``geometric side'' and the ``analytic side'' of CFT, via $\Embc$ and $\Ind\Hilb$, aimed at relating these different aspects of QFT.


We note that, in dimension $2$, the boundedness criterion is more subtle and appears to be related to the universal Teichm\"uller space, potentially in connection with Segal's functorial CFT (see \cite{MBergman} for two-dimensional examples).

Segal's axioms for functorial CFT provide a foundational paradigm for treating CFT as a functor out of bordism categories \cite{Segal}. Huang's approach to two-dimensional CFT, which emphasizes sewing operations and their realization in the language of vertex operator algebras, offers another closely related perspective \cite{Huang}. These viewpoints also motivate the geometric formalism developed here. The relationship between our category $\Disk$ and the bordism categories appearing in the frameworks of Segal, Huang, and Stolz--Teichner will be discussed in forthcoming work \cite{Mvertex}. Further connections with factorization algebras and vertex operator algebras are treated in \cite{Mfactorization,MBergman}. In the introduction, we first review the relevant background and then present our main results.






























































































\vspace{3mm}

\textbf{Geometric and analytic aspects of quantum field theory}

Our definitions are closely related to, and motivated by, factorization algebras \cite{BD,CG1,CG2}.
Factorization algebras provide a geometric formalism for QFTs that are not necessarily topological.
In particular, the factorization algebra of the free scalar field gives a symmetric monoidal functor
from the category of Riemannian manifolds and isometric open embeddings 
to the category of (differentiable) vector spaces \cite[Chapter 6.3]{CG1}.



On the other hand, the mathematical study of QFT also has a real-analytic aspect,
in which Hilbert spaces and unitary operators play an important role.
For instance, the G\r{a}rding-Wightman axioms are formulated in terms of a Hilbert space $\Hf$,
a projective unitary representation of the Poincar\'e group $\R^d \rtimes \mathrm{SO}^+(d-1,1)$ on $\Hf$,
and operator-valued tempered distributions (quantum fields), which are typically {unbounded} \cite{GW,SW}.
Since the Poincar\'e group is the isometry group of the pseudo-Riemannian manifold $\R^{d-1,1}$,
one may view axiomatic QFT as encoding, via Hilbert space data, a local flat geometric structure
analogous to that captured by a (topological) d-disk algebra.


While factorization algebras reveal an elegant link between QFT and geometry, many known examples
are constructed perturbatively, i.e.\ over the formal power series $\C[[h]]$.
It is well known that perturbative expansions in QFT typically have zero radius of convergence
\cite{Dy,Jaffe}, and extracting analytic QFT from perturbation theory remains under development
(see, for example, \cite{Marino}).
In contrast, real-analytic approaches naturally lead to non-formal analytic structures, such as
the probabilistic (measure-theoretic) construction of path integrals by Glimm-Jaffe
\cite{Glimm-Jaffe}, as well as frameworks involving Hilbert spaces, unitary representations,
and von Neumann algebras (see \cite{Haag,Araki, Simon}).
Many results in this direction are local constructions on flat spacetime, and it is a nontrivial
problem to assemble given local data to global data.


















\vspace{3mm}


\textbf{Conformally flat Riemannian manifolds and left Kan extensions}

When passing from local to global, Riemannian geometry is extremely rigid; the only flat Riemannian manifolds that exist are
the direct product of Euclidean spaces and the torus.
Consequently, even if one starts from a single local model as in axiomatic QFT, 
the class of manifolds accessible by taking its left Kan extension is highly limited.
In contrast, conformal geometry is much more flexible.
Every two-dimensional Riemannian manifold is conformally flat via isothermal coordinates \cite{Che55}, and in three dimensions, three of Thurston's geometric models -- Euclidean geometry, spherical geometry, and hyperbolic geometry -- possess structures as conformally flat manifolds \cite{Thurston, Kuiper, Kuiper2}.




For this reason, we consider QFTs with conformal symmetry (conformal field theories, CFTs) rather than general QFTs. Our question is: ``How can we lift a local/analytic CFT, described by a Hilbert space, onto a global/geometric theory (factorization algebra)?'' The view of factorization homology suggests that ``the local/analytic CFT described by a Hilbert space is the coefficient ring of a homology-type theory, and the global/geometric theory is obtained as its left Kan extension.''

A major difficulty in placing analytic QFT into a categorical framework is that,
as in the G\r{a}rding-Wightman axioms, quantum fields are {unbounded operators} even after smearing by test functions,
which makes their categorical treatment difficult. This analytic difficulty seems to reflect a corresponding geometric difficulty as discussed below.


















\vspace{3mm}

\textbf{Higher commutativity and unitary representations}

In the topological setting, a $d$-disk algebra is an algebraic object that encodes,
in a homotopy-theoretic way, the dimension-dependent ``noncommutativity'' of geometry---
much as the entanglement of knots in three dimensions reflects genuinely three-dimensional behavior.
This noncommutativity gives rise to manifold invariants that depend on the dimension.
QFT on (Minkowski) spacetime also comes equipped with a dimension-dependent (non)commutativity
of quantum fields $\phi(x)$, called locality:
\begin{align*}
\text{(Locality)}\qquad\qquad
[\phi(x),\phi(y)]=0, \quad \text{``if $(x_0-y_0)^2 < \sum_{i=1}^{d-1} (x_i-y_i)^2$.''}
\end{align*}

Physically, this expresses that information cannot propagate faster than the speed of light (a.k.a., \emph{causality}) \cite{GW,SW}.
Locality is one of the most nontrivial conditions in the G\r{a}rding--Wightman axioms, and constructing
examples beyond free fields that satisfy it in dimension $\geq 4$ remains an open problem to this day.
Therefore, in geometric factorization homology theory, constructing concrete examples of $\Disk$-algebras---the conformal
Riemannian analogues of disk algebras---is an essential part of the subject.

One advantage of working with $\Disk$-algebras valued in ind Hilbert spaces is that natural examples
 yield unitary representations of $\SO^+(d,1)$ on Hilbert spaces (see below).
From a representation-theoretic viewpoint, $\Disk$-algebras provide, via category theory,
a mechanism that relates unitary representations of conformal diffeomorphism groups
to conformal Riemannian geometry.
We expect this perspective to be closely connected to the theory of unitary representations
and its connection to conformal geometry developed by Kobayashi--{\O}rsted \cite{KO1,KO2,KO3}.





















In what follows, we present the main results of this paper: the formulation of
$\Disk$-algebras, the computation of their left Kan extensions, and the construction of
concrete examples.






















































\vspace{3mm}

\noindent
\begin{center}
{\bf 0.1. Riemannian manifolds, conformal embeddings, and disk algebras}
\end{center}


 

As a conformal-geometric analogue of factorization homology \eqref{eq_intro_left},
we consider the category $\Emb$ whose objects are oriented $d$-dimensional
Riemannian manifolds without boundary and whose morphisms are
orientation-preserving conformal open embeddings.
Let $\bD_d=\{x\in\R^d \mid |x|<1\}$ be the unit disk equipped with the standard
Riemannian metric $g_{\std}=dx_1^2+\cdots+dx_d^2$, and denote by $\tDisk$
the full monoidal subcategory of $\Emb$ generated by the unit disk.

\begin{figure}[h]
\begin{minipage}[c]{.44\textwidth}
\centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.3]{operad4.jpg}
    \caption{$\CE$-operad}\label{fig_intro_operad}
\end{minipage}
\begin{minipage}[l]{.50\textwidth}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[scale=0.18]{operad_module.jpg}
    \caption{Kan extension}\label{fig_intro_module}
\end{minipage}
\end{figure}
This category defines an operad of conformal open embeddings of disks
(Fig. \ref{fig_intro_operad})
\begin{align*}
\CEc(n)
&=\mathrm{Hom}_{\Emb}(\sqcup_n \bD_d,\bD_d),\qquad\qquad\qquad (n \geq 0)\\
&=\left\{\phi:\underbrace{\bD_d \sqcup \cdots \sqcup \bD_d}_n \rightarrow \bD_d
\;\middle|\;\text{orientation-preserving conformal open embeddings} \right\},
\end{align*}
which gives a naive definition of a $d$-disk operad in conformally flat geometry (to be refined later). The choice of the unit disk as the local model is motivated by the relation between vertex operator algebras and analytic QFT \cite{CKLW,AGT,AMT} (see below).














In two dimensions, orientation-preserving conformal maps and holomorphic maps with nonzero derivatives are equivalent, and $\CEct(1)$ is the infinite-dimensional monoid of all injective holomorphic maps from disk to disk.
On the other hand, for $d \geq 3$, Liouville's theorem \cite{Liouville} implies that any conformal map from disk to disk can be expressed as a restriction of the group $\Conf(S^d) \cong \mathrm{O}^+(d+1,1)$, which consists of all conformal diffeomorphisms on the standard sphere.
In particular, for $d \geq 3$, the monoid $\CEc(1)$ can be identified as
$\CEc(1)=\{g \in \SO^+(d+1,1)\mid g(\bD_d) \subset \bD_d\}$, and $\CEc(n)$ consists of 
all conformal disk embeddings whose images are mutually disjoint
\begin{align}
\CEc(n)
= \{(f_1,\dots,f_n) \in \CEc(1)^n \mid f_i(\bD_d)\cap f_j(\bD_d)=\emptyset \}.
\label{eq_intro_geom}
\end{align}
Note that \eqref{eq_intro_geom} allows configurations such as $\overline{f_i(\bD_d)}\cap\overline{f_j(\bD_d)} \neq \emptyset$.
Our key observation is that, in the natural construction of a $\CEc$-algebra associated with the free scalar field,
such configurations give rise to unbounded operators on Hilbert spaces (Theorem \ref{thm_unbounded})














To address this issue, we introduce a category $\Embc$ whose objects are triples $(U,g,\overline{M})$, where $\overline{M}$ is a compact Riemannian manifold which may have boundary (called the \emph{core}),
and $U$ is an open Riemannian neighborhood of $\overline{M}$, \emph{a germ of a Riemannian manifold}.
Morphisms in $\Embc$ are given by orientation-preserving conformal open embeddings
that are defined on neighborhoods of the cores.
The germ of the closed disk $\overline{\bD}_d$ in flat Euclidean space defines an object of $\Embc$.
We denote by $\Disk$ the full monoidal subcategory of $\Embc$ generated by $\overline{\bD}_d$.
The morphisms in $\Disk$ refine \eqref{eq_intro_geom} to
\begin{align}
\CE(n)
= \Hom_{\Embc}(\sqcup_n \overline{\bD}_d,\overline{\bD}_d)
=
\{(f_1,\dots,f_n) \in \CE(1)^n \mid \overline{f_i(\bD_d)}\cap \overline{f_j(\bD_d)} = \emptyset \}.
\label{eq_intro_geom2}
\end{align}



Here, $\CE(1)$ coincides with $\CEc(1)$ for $d \geq 3$, but for $d=2$, it becomes genuinely smaller because an injective holomorphic map $f:\bD_2 \rightarrow \bD_2$ may not extend to a neighborhood of $\overline{\bD}_2$.
Our key result is that for $d \geq 3$ and for the basic examples, the necessary and sufficient condition for the operators associated to elements of $\CEc(n)$ to be bounded is precisely when they lie in $\CE(n) \subset \CEc(n)$ (Theorem \ref{thm_unbounded}). (In dimension two this situation is more subtle; see \cite{MBergman}.)


We call  this operad a \textbf{conformally flat d-disk operad}.

(Our definition is also motivated by the definition of functorial CFT by Stolz and Teichner;
see Remark~\ref{rem_cobordism}.)

Let $\cC$ be a cocomplete symmetric monoidal category with a colimit distributive tensor product.
A $\CE$-algebra in $\cC$ is equivalently a symmetric monoidal functor $\Disk \rightarrow \cC$,
and its (1-categorical) left Kan extension
\begin{equation}
\label{eq_intro_left2}
\begin{tikzcd}
\Disk
  \arrow[r,"A"]
  \arrow[d,hook,"i"']
& 
\mathcal{C}
\\
\Embc
  \arrow[ru,dashed,"\mathrm{Lan}_{A}"']
& 
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
defines a symmetric monoidal functor $\mathrm{Lan}_A: \Embc \rightarrow \cC$ (see Proposition~\ref{prop_Left_Kan}).
In particular, this yields invariants for conformally flat manifolds.


































When $\cC$ is the category of vector spaces $\Vect$, a $\CE$-algebra gives a collection
of products on a vector space $A$ compatible with the operadic composition (Fig. \ref{fig_intro_operad})
\begin{align}
\rho_n: \CE(n) \rightarrow \Hom_\Vect(A^{\otimes n},A),
\qquad\qquad (n \geq 0).
\label{eq_intro_product}
\end{align}
In this case, for each object $\overline{M} \in \Embc$, giving
$\chi \in \mathrm{Hom}_\Vect\bigl(\mathrm{Lan}_A(\overline{M}),\C\bigr)$
is equivalent to giving a family of maps
\begin{align}
\chi_n:
\mathrm{Hom}_{\Embc}(\sqcup_n \overline{\bD},\overline{M})
\rightarrow
\Hom_{\Vect}(A^{\otimes n},\C),
\qquad\qquad (n \geq 0),
\label{eq_intro_state}
\end{align}
which is compatible with the $\CE$-operad action
(Fig. \ref{fig_intro_module})
\begin{align}
\CE(n) \times
\mathrm{Hom}_{\Embc}(\sqcup_m \overline{\bD},\overline{M})
\rightarrow
\mathrm{Hom}_{\Embc}(\sqcup_{n+m-1} \overline{\bD},\overline{M}).
\label{eq_intro_ope}
\end{align}
We call such a collection $\chi$ a \emph{state} of $A$ on $\overline{M}$.
When $\overline{M}$ has no boundary, we expect a state to recover the \emph{partition function} on $\overline{M}$ in physics,
while for manifolds with boundary, states should encode the boundary degrees of freedom of the CFT.

In the theory of vertex operator algebras, the compatibility condition
\eqref{eq_intro_ope} is naturally interpreted as compatibility with the operator product expansion,
appearing in the definition of conformal blocks \cite{TUY,Zh}.
From this viewpoint, the left Kan extension \eqref{eq_intro_left2} can be viewed as
a Riemannian-geometric analogue of conformal blocks and chiral homology \cite{BD},
without {relying on holomorphicity}.



































 




\vspace{3mm}

\noindent
\begin{center}
{0.2. \bf 
$\CE$-algebra in Ind Hilbert space
}
\end{center}

A \emph{Hilbert space filtration} of a $\CE$-algebra $A$ in $\Vect$ is a family of subspaces
\begin{align*}
A = \cup_{k=0}^\infty H^k, \qquad H^0 \subset H^1 \subset H^2 \subset \dots
\end{align*}
such that each $H^k$ is a Hilbert space and the inclusion $H^k \subset H^{k+1}$ is isometric, and
each $H^k$ is preserved by the action of the monoid $\CE(1)$, and
for any $n \geq 1$ and $k_1,\dots,k_n \geq 0$, there exists $K \geq 0$ such that the restriction of \eqref{eq_intro_product} to $H^{k_1}\otimes \cdots \otimes H^{k_n} \subset A^{\otimes n}$ has image contained in $H^K$, and defines a bounded operator between Hilbert spaces
\footnote{
In Hilbert spaces, a continuous bilinear map $H_1 \times H_2 \rightarrow H$ and
a bounded operator $H_1 \hotimes H_2 \rightarrow H$ from the completed tensor product are not the same notion.
In this paper we adopt the latter, as required by the definition of the tensor product in the category of ind-Hilbert spaces $\Ind \Hilb$ (see Definition \ref{def_ind_CF}).}.


Let $\Hilb$ be the category of separable Hilbert spaces and bounded operators, and let $\Ind \Hilb$ be its ind category.
A $\CE$-algebra $A$ equipped with a Hilbert space filtration naturally determines a symmetric monoidal functor
$\Disk \rightarrow \Ind \Hilb$.
The filtration is important because it allows one to impose continuity on \eqref{eq_intro_state} and thereby
to introduce natural notions such as continuous states and closed ideals of $A$.
We call a $\CE$-algebra \emph{simple} if it has no nontrivial closed ideals.

We are interested in when the continuous states (partition functions) obtained via left Kan extension are nontrivial.
Given a continuous state $\chi_M$ on $\overline{M}$, define its \emph{radical} $N(\chi_M)$ to be the subspace of $A$
consisting of those $v \in A$ such that
\begin{align*}
\chi_M(\iota)(a_1,\dots,a_{n-1},v)=0
\end{align*}
for any $n \geq 1$, $\iota \in \mathrm{Hom}_{\Embc}(\sqcup_n \overline{\bD},\overline{M})$ and
$a_1,\dots,a_{n-1} \in A$.
By continuity of the state, $N(\chi_M)$ is a closed ideal. Hence, if $A$ is simple then any non-zero continuous state on a conformally flat manifold has trivial radical (Proposition \ref{prop_null_ideal}).










Among the states of $A$ on $\overline{\bD}$ there is a distinguished one, called the \emph{vacuum expectation value} in physics.
Under additional assumptions to be stated later, simplicity of $A$ can be determined by the triviality of the radical of the vacuum expectation value (Proposition \ref{prop_criterion_simple}).
This can be regarded as an analogue of Cartan's criterion for Lie algebras.


































\vspace{3mm}


\noindent
\begin{center}
{0.3. \bf Reflection positivity in conformal field theory}
\end{center}

Here we explain why we choose the unit disk as the local geometric model.
In topological quantum field theory, unitarity can be incorporated by equipping the
cobordism category with a dagger structure \cite{Atiyah,Steh2}.
In our setting, it is natural not only to take the target category to be
$\Ind \Hilb$, but also to impose additional positivity conditions.

In Euclidean QFT, the natural notion of positivity is
Osterwalder--Schrader reflection positivity \cite{OS1,OS2}, which is defined by choosing an involution of $\R^d$,
\begin{align*}
\R^d \rightarrow \R^d,\quad (x_1,x_2,\dots,x_d) \mapsto (-x_1,x_2,\dots,x_d).
\end{align*}
The choice of involution is non-canonical; however, any two such choices are equivalent by
$\SO(d)$-symmetry.
On the other hand, for vertex operator algebras (VOA) \cite{B1,FLM}, which provide a formulation
of two-dimensional chiral CFT, unitarity is defined by the involution
$z \mapsto \bar z^{-1}$ \cite{FHL,Li,DL}.
For a unitary chiral VOA $V$, its underlying vector space admits a Hilbert space completion
$\overline{V}^{\mathrm{Hilb}}$, and Carpi-Kawahigashi-Longo-Weiner showed that
(Minkowskian) Wightman fields on $S^1=\{z \in \C \mid z = \z^{-1} \}$ acts on $\overline{V}^{\mathrm{Hilb}}$ \cite{CKLW}.

Moreover, Adamo-Tanimoto and the author showed that Osterwalder--Schrader reflection positivity
corresponds to the unitarity of full VOAs \cite{AMT}.

Under the Wick rotation $t\mapsto i\tau$, Minkowski spacetime
$e^{it+i\theta}\in S^1\times S^1$ is mapped to $e^{-\tau+i\theta}\in \bD_2$
\cite{AGT,AMT}.
From this perspective, our local model $\Disk$ generated by $\bD_d$
 is naturally associated with the reflection positivity of the inversion
\begin{align}
J_{\mathrm{RP}}:\R^d \rightarrow \R^d,\quad (x_1,x_2,\dots,x_d) \mapsto
\left(\frac{x_1}{\norm{x}^2},\frac{x_2}{\norm{x}^2},\dots,\frac{x_d}{\norm{x}^2}\right).
\label{eq_intro_reflection}
\end{align}


We expect that such considerations should be derived from an appropriate
definition of a dagger structure on the geometric category, but we leave this direction
to future work.

The choice of reflection positivity \eqref{eq_intro_reflection} also suggests that
Hermitian conditions should be imposed on the products of a $\CE$-algebra.
In this paper, we impose the following conditions on the monoid homomorphism
$\rho_1:\CE(1)\rightarrow \mathrm{End}\,A$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(U)]
The restriction of $\rho_1$ to 
$\{f\in \CE(1)\mid J_{\mathrm{RP}} f J_{RP}^{-1} =f \}\cong \SO^+(d,1)$
defines a strongly continuous unitary representation on $H^k$ for each $k\geq 0$.
\item[(D)]
Dilations, shrinking the disk by a factor $r\in(0,1)$, define a strongly continuous,
self-adjoint, contractive semigroup representation by Hilbert-Schmidt operators.\footnote{
The assumption that these operators are Hilbert--Schmidt is quite strong; physically,
it corresponds to restricting attention to so-called \emph{compact CFTs} (see also \cite{M1}).}
\end{enumerate}
Note that the dilation $D(r)$ satisfies $J_{\mathrm{RP}} D(r) J_{RP}^{-1} = D(r^{-1})$.

By condition (D), the $\CE$-algebra $A$ decomposes into the eigenspaces for dilations (Proposition \ref{prop_semigroup}),
\begin{align}
A=\bigoplus_{\Delta\geq 0} A_\Delta,
\label{eq_intro_eigenspace}
\end{align}
where each $A_\Delta$ is finite-dimensional (in CFT, $\Delta$ is
called the conformal dimension).
In this situation, we obtain the following result (Theorem \ref{cor_left_nonzero}):
\begin{thm}\label{thm_left_nonzero}
Let $A$ be a $\CE$-algebra equipped with a Hilbert space filtration satisfying (U) and (D) with $\dim A_0=1$.
Then the left Kan extension to the sphere $(S^d,g_\std)$ satisfies
\begin{align}
\dim \Hom_{\Ind \Hilb}(\mathrm{Lan}_A(S^d,g_\std),\C)^{\Conf^+(S^d,g_\std)} = 1.
\label{eq_intro_coh}
\end{align}
Here \eqref{eq_intro_coh} denotes the fixed subspace under the action of the
orientation-preserving conformal diffeomorphism group $\Conf^+(S^d,g_\std)$.
\end{thm}

In other words, there exists a unique $\Conf^+(S^d,g_\std)$-invariant partition function on the standard sphere $S^d$,
and it is obtained via left Kan extension.
Motivated by Zhu's results for VOAs \cite{Zh}, we expect that states on conformally flat
manifolds such as $S^1\times S^{d-1}$ are classified by representations of $A$, and that,
correspondingly, $\mathrm{Lan}_A(S^1\times S^{d-1})$ encodes the representation-theoretic
information of $A$.
























































\vspace{3mm}

\noindent
\begin{center}
{0.4. \bf 
Example from harmonic analysis
}
\end{center}

The construction of non-simple $\CE$-algebras is straightforward (see Section \ref{sec_ind_Hilb}), whereas the construction of
simple $\CE$-algebras is highly nontrivial.
Providing concrete examples of simple $\CE$-algebras is one of the central goals of this paper.
In this work, we construct examples of $\Disk$-algebras in dimensions $d \geq 3$.
For $d=2$ case, we refer to \cite{MBergman}.
From the viewpoint of factorization algebras, these examples can be understood as
completions, as ind Hilbert spaces, of the factorization algebra associated with the conformal Laplacian.
This perspective is discussed in \cite{Mfactorization}.

Our construction is based on the vector space of harmonic polynomials in $d$ variables,




whose completion with respect to a suitable inner product yields a real Hilbert space $\Hf$.
This space admits a realization as a reproducing kernel Hilbert space on the disk,
and carries a unitary representation of $\SO^+(d,1)$.
Using the reproducing kernel structure, we obtain a representation of the monoid $\CE(1)$
satisfying conditions (U) and (D),
\begin{align*}
\rho_1: \CE(1) \rightarrow \mathbb{B}(\Hf),
\end{align*}
where $\mathbb{B}(\Hf)$ denotes the $C^*$-algebra of bounded operators on $\Hf$ (Proposition \ref{prop_unitary}).

The nontrivial part of the construction lies in defining the higher products and proving their boundedness.
For any conformal disk embedding $(f_1,f_2) \in \CE(2)$, we construct a bounded operator
from the Hilbert space tensor product,
\begin{align}
C_{f_1,f_2}:\Hf \hat{\otimes} \Hf \rightarrow \R,
\label{eq_intro_contraction}
\end{align}
which satisfies, for any $g,h_1,h_2 \in \CE(1)$,
\begin{align}
C_{g f_1, g f_2}=C_{f_1,f_2}, \qquad
C_{f_1 h_1, f_2 h_2}
= C_{f_1,f_2}\circ (\rho_1(h_1)\otimes \rho_1(h_2))
\label{eq_intro_cont_inv}
\end{align}
(see Theorem \ref{thm_wick_contraction}).


The construction proceeds as follows:
(I) we define $C_{f_1,f_2}$ formally as a linear map on a dense subspace of $\Hf$;
(II) we show that, when $f_1$ and $f_2$ are given by compositions of dilations and translations,
this operator admits a bounded closure;
(III) using \eqref{eq_intro_cont_inv}, we extend boundedness to arbitrary elements of $\CE(2)$.

Step (II) is the key observation: it shows that operators which are a priori unbounded, as is typical for quantum fields, become genuinely bounded under suitable geometric separation conditions \eqref{eq_intro_geom2} encoded in $\CE$. On the other hand, for an element of the naive operad $(f_1,f_2) \in \CEc(2) \setminus \CE(2)$ in \eqref{eq_intro_geom}, we prove that $C_{f_1,f_2}$ is an unbounded operator (Theorem \ref{thm_unbounded}). These results are proved by directly estimating the norm of $C_{f_1,f_2}$ using a combinatorial description of the inner product on harmonic polynomials (Theorem \ref{thm_bounded}).
Using this geometric generalization of Wick contraction, we obtain
a nontrivial $\CE$-algebra structure on $\Sym \Hf = \bigoplus_{n\geq 0}\Sym \Hf$
 equipped with a Hilbert space filtration
(Theorem \ref{thm_CF}).





































\vspace{3mm}

\noindent
\begin{center}
{0.5. \bf 
Relation with functorial CFT and future perspectives
}
\end{center}



An element of $\CE$ may be viewed as a bordism obtained by excising disks from the sphere.
From this perspective, our notion of $\CE$-algebras is closely related to the conformal bootstrap
\cite{Poly,FGG,BPZ,RRTV,EPPRSV}, functorial CFT \cite{Segal,ST}, and the (partial) operads arising from CFTs
defined via bordisms \cite{Huang,Schwartz}.
However, in a cobordism category such morphisms do not form a full subcategory, which makes the
formulation of left Kan extensions less suitable.
Moreover, gluing along conformal maps requires working with conformal classes of Riemannian metrics, in which case the resulting algebraic structure becomes projective \cite{Segal,ST,Huang}, whereas in our setting the objects are Riemannian manifolds themselves.
These relation will be discussed in more detail in the forthcoming paper \cite{Mvertex}.















Finally, we briefly mention several directions for future work that we could not address in this paper.
We do not discuss fundamental properties of factorization homology such as $\otimes$-excision.
As noted in Section 0.3, strong continuity and unitarity should ideally be derived from additional categorical
structures, such as a dagger structure and an enrichment.
Our work was originally motivated by the idea of constructing Segal's functorial CFT via a homology-type theory, in a spirit analogous to the relationship between factorization homology and topological quantum field theory. Constructing functorial CFTs in Segal's sense is difficult: there are constructions for the free field due to Tener \cite{Te}, and probabilistic constructions in non-compact settings \cite{DKRV,KRV,GKRV1,GKRV2}, but no examples are known for non-free compact CFTs.
In two dimensions, many examples of compact CFTs are known \cite{FRS,HK1,M1,MSwiss}, and it is natural to expect that one can
construct various non-free $\CEt$-algebras as their completions (see \cite{MBergman}).
Such constructions are expected to be closely related to the representation theory of infinite-dimensional semigroups
developed by Henriques--Tener \cite{HT1,HT2}.
Since local conformal maps are abundant in two dimensions by the Riemann mapping theorem,
the left Kan extension should have good properties.
An important problem is whether one can construct functorial CFT as a homology theory with a unitary full vertex operator
algebra as coefficients.



















































\vspace{3mm}

The organization of this paper is as follows.
In Section~\ref{sec_operad}, we set up the geometric framework and define $\CE$-algebras.
Section~\ref{sec_category} introduces the refined category $\Embc$ with the full subcategory $\Disk$ and its left Kan extension.
In Section~\ref{sec_conf_sphere}, we study the group of conformal transformations of the standard
sphere $S^d$ and its relevant submonoids.
Section~\ref{sec_def_CF} gives an explicit description of the operad $\CE$ associated with $\Disk$.
In Section~\ref{sec_ind_Hilb}, we introduce Hilbert space filtrations on $\CE$-algebras and impose
the Hermitian conditions (U) and (D) on monoid actions, and study their consequences.
Concrete examples are constructed in Section~\ref{sec_example}.
Section~\ref{sec_infinitesimal} reviews the action of the Lie algebra $\sod$ on harmonic
polynomials, and Section~\ref{sec_reproduce} constructs the action of $\CE(1)$ on
their Hilbert space completions.
Section~\ref{sec_inequality} contains the norm estimate, which is the most technical results of the paper; based on these,
$\CE$-algebras are constructed in Sections~\ref{sec_completion} and~\ref{sec_construction}.
In Section~\ref{sec_left}, we study left Kan extensions using the notion of continuous states.
After briefly discussing the case of $\Vect$ in Section~\ref{sec_left_state}, we analyze in
Section~\ref{sec_conti_state} the relationship between continuous states, closed ideals, and
simplicity of $\CE$-algebras.
Section~\ref{sec_correlation} constructs conformally invariant states on the sphere and shows how
partition functions arise from left Kan extensions to $S^d$.
Sections~\ref{sec_left} and~\ref{sec_example} are logically independent.
Appendix~\ref{app_remark} collects basic results on the action of $\Conf^+(S^d)$ on $S^d$, while
Appendix~\ref{app_Hilb} reviews standard facts about the category of Hilbert spaces and its ind
category.














