	\title{Discovering hypergeometric series with harmonic numbers via Wilf-Zeilberger seeds}
	
	\author[Kam Cheong Au]{Kam Cheong Au}
	
	\address{University of Cologne \\ Department of Mathematics and Computer Science \\ Weyertal 86-90, 50931 Cologne, Germany} 

\email{kau@uni-koeln.de}
	\subjclass[2020]{Primary: 11B65, 11M32, 33C20. Secondary: 33F10}
	
	\keywords{Accelerating series, Harmonic number, Hypergeometric series, Multiple zeta values, Wilf-Zeilberger pair} 
	
	
	
	\begin{abstract}By extracting coefficients from Wilf–Zeilberger pairs with respect to auxiliary parameters, we discover many nontrivial hypergeometric series involving harmonic numbers. In particular, we obtain a rapidly convergent series for the depth-two multiple zeta value $\zeta(5,3)$, which appears to be the first result of its kind in the literature. We also experiment with the Hilbert–Poincaré series attached with a WZ-seed and conjecture that it admits a remarkably simple form, suggesting the presence of an underlying graded algebra structure behind WZ-seeds.
	\end{abstract}
	
	\maketitle
	\section{Introduction}
	
	A \textit{Wilf-Zeilberger (WZ) pair} \cite{AequalsB, wilf1990rational} consists of a pair of two-variable functions $F,G$ satisfying
	\begin{equation}\label{WZ}
		F(n+1,k) - F(n,k) = G(n,k+1) - G(n,k).
	\end{equation}
	Originally introduced in the context of combinatorial summation identities, the WZ-method has since found wide applications in the discovery and rigorous proof of hypergeometric series that are otherwise inaccessible to classical techniques \cite{mohammed2005infinite, pilehrood2011bivariate, pilehrood2010series, pilehrood2008simultaneous, guillera2008hypergeometric, guillera2018dougall, guillera2003new, sun2024new}. A prime example is the fast-converging series for Apéry’s constant \cite{amdeberhan1998hypergeometric}:
	\begin{equation}\label{aux_0}
		\sum_{n\geq 1} \left(\frac{-1}{2^{10}}\right)^n \frac{(1)_n^5}{(\frac12)_n^5} \frac{32-160 n+205 n^2}{n^5} = -2\zeta(3),
	\end{equation}
	here $(a)_n := a(a+1)\cdots (a+n-1)$ is the rising factorial.\par
	
The theory of WZ-pairs has recently undergone a leap with the introduction of the notion of a \textit{WZ-seed} by the author \cite{au2025wilf}. This concept, which already appeared implicitly (though unnamed) in earlier work of Gessel \cite{gessel1995finding}. It allows a systematic construction of WZ-pairs, whereas educated guessing had previously been the only available approach. This immediately proves some long-standing conjectural evaluations such as
		\begin{align}\label{aux_7}\sum_{n\geq 1} \left(-\frac{2^{10}}{5^5}\right)^n \frac{(1)_n^9}{(\frac12)_n^5 (\frac15)_n(\frac25)_n(\frac35)_n(\frac45)_n} \frac{30-425 n+2275 n^2-5600 n^3+5532 n^4 }{n^9} &= -380928 \zeta(5), \\
			\sum_{n\geq 0} \left(\frac{1}{2^6}\right)^n \frac{(\frac12)_n^7}{(1)_n^7} (168 n^3+76 n^2+14 n+1) &= \frac{32}{\pi^3},
		\end{align}
	which were conjectured by Zhao and Gourevich respectively. Another advantage of the WZ-seed language is that they come with auxiliary parameters - denoted throughout this article by $a,b,c,d,e$. By coefficient extraction, these parameters lead to a rich collection of extensions of the underlying hypergeometric series, often involving harmonic numbers. Several simple instances of this phenomenon were conjectured by Sun \cite{sun2022conjectures,sun2010conjectures,sun2024new,sun2013products,sun2023new}. In such extensions, \textit{multiple zeta values} (MZVs) arise naturally. We use the  notation
	$$\zeta(s_1,\cdots,s_k) := \sum_{n_1 > \cdots > n_k \geq 1} \frac{1}{n_1^{s_1}\cdots n_k^{s_k}},$$
	and as standard practice, we put a bar on $s_i$ to indicate alternating sum on the corresponding index. We denote $\MZV{}{n}$ as the $\mathbb{Q}$-span of all MZVs of weight $n$. \par
	
	Among a plethora of identities we prove or conjecture in this article, we highlight two notable instances (Examples \ref{Ex_3}, \ref{Ex_4}):
	$$\begin{aligned}&\sum_{n\geq 1}  \left(\frac{-1}{27}\right)^n \frac{(1)_n^2}{(\frac13)_n (\frac23)_n} \frac{17-136 n+408 n^2-640 n^3+560 n^4}{n^5 (2n-1)^4} = 180 \zeta (5)-\frac{56 \pi ^2 \zeta (3)}{3}, \\ &\sum_{n\geq 1} \left(\frac{-1}{2^{10}}\right)^n \frac{(1)_n^5}{(\frac12)_n^5} \left(\frac{351-1755 n+2240 n^2}{n^{10}}-\frac{\left(11 H_n^{(5)}-H_{2 n}^{(5)}\right) (205 n^2-160 n+32)}{n^5}\right) = -4 \zeta(5,3)-\frac{\pi ^8}{4725},\end{aligned}$$ 
	the first were conjectured by Sun. Both enable high-precision evaluation of the constants on their RHSs. The reader may recognize the second identity as an extension of equation~\eqref{aux_0}, it furnishes a fast-converging series for a MZV of \textit{depth greater than one}, it appears to be the first example of its kind in the literature. \par

It is natural to ask why higher-depth MZVs appear, their origin actually lies in a new and intriguing connection of certain very-well-poised $_7F_6$ hypergeometric series to MZVs. Specifically, consider the hypergeometric series (Examples \ref{Ex_1}, \ref{Ex_3})
	$$f(a,b,c,d,e) := \sum_{k\geq 0} \frac{(a+2 e+2 k+2) (a+e+1)_k (b+e+1)_k (c+e+1)_k (d+e+1)_k}{(e+1)_{k+1}(a-b+e+1)_{k+1} (a-c+e+1)_{k+1} (a-d+e+1)_{k+1}},$$
	it defines an analytic function around the point $(a,b,c,d,e) = (0,0,0,0,0)$, if we write $c(i_1,\cdots,i_5)$ as the corresponding power series coefficient, $$f(a,b,c,d,e) = \sum_{i_1,\cdots,i_5\geq 0}  c(i_1,\cdots,i_5) a^{i_1} b^{i_2} \cdots e^{i_5},$$
	then it can be shown that $c(i_1,\cdots,i_5) \in \MZV{}{2+i_1+\cdots+i_5}$. The $\mathbb{Q}$-span of coefficients with fixed weight seems to have intrinsic interest: not only do these coefficients describe all constants attainable by such fast-converging series, but they also motivate the following conjecture.
	\begin{conjecture}
	Let $$V_n := \text{Span}_\mathbb{Q}\{c(i_1,\cdots,i_5) | i_1+\cdots+i_5 = n\},$$
	then its Hilbert-Poincaré series is a rational function, more precisely,
	$$\sum_{n=0}^\infty \dim_\mathbb{Q} V_n t^n \stackrel{?}{\in} \mathbb{Q}(t).$$
	\end{conjecture}
	
	This conjecture, reminiscent of Zagier’s conjecture \cite{gil2017multiple} on $\dim \MZV{}{n}$, suggests the presence of a deeper underlying algebraic structure. Presence of depth 2 MZV in $V_n$ would also tell us $f(a,b,c,d,e)$ cannot be summed in closed-form (i.e. it is not a gamma product). An entirely analogous discussion applies when $f(a,b,c,d,e)$ is replaced with (Example \ref{Ex_2})
	$$\sum_{k\geq 0} \frac{(a+2 e+2 k+2) (a+e+1)_k (b+e+1)_k (c+e+1)_k (d+e+\frac{1}{2})_{k+1}}{(e+1)_{k+1} (a-b+e+1)_{k+1} (a-c+e+1)_{k+1} (a-d+e+\frac{1}{2})_{k+1}}.$$
	~\\[0.03in]
	Another theme of this article is the discovery of further evaluations arising from coefficient extraction applied to WZ seeds. The WZ relation~\eqref{WZ} implies the summation formula
	\begin{equation}\label{aux_8}\sum_{k\geq 0} F(0,k) = \sum_{n\geq 0} G(0,n) \qquad \text{(up to simpler terms)}.\end{equation}
	The $f(a,b,c,d,e)$ above arises naturally as $F(0,k)$ of a WZ-seed. Typically, $\sum_{k\geq 0} F(0,k)$ is straightforward to handle whereas $\sum_{n\geq 0} G(0,n)$ is the more non-trivial and interesting part. When the WZ seed involves auxiliary parameters $a,b,c,\cdots$, these parameters also appear in $G(0,n)$, and extracting appropriate coefficients allows one to predict, \textit{a priori}, which series admit closed-form evaluations. \par 
	
	We illustrate this strategy through explicit examples in Section~4. The sheer number of identities obtained by this method might be difficult to discover, either rigorously or conjecturally, without the WZ-seed framework. In particular, we obtain several extensions of the remarkable $\zeta(5)$-formula~\eqref{aux_7} in Example \ref{Ex_10}. \par
	
	We also note that WZ-seeds appear to exhibit a remarkably regular Hilbert-Poincaré structure when expanded about a base point in the parameter space. More precisely, expanding $G(0,n)$ around a certain point $(a,b,c,d,e)\in \mathbb{Q}^5$ and considering the space $V_N$ spanned by all coefficients of total degree $N$. For the five-parameter extension of formula~\eqref{aux_0}, we conjecture (see Example~\ref{Ex_3} and its remark)
		$$\sum_{N\geq 0} \dim V_N t^N \stackrel{?}{=} \frac{1}{(1-t)(1-t^2)(1-t^3)(1-t^4)(1-t^5)}.$$
	
	This suggests that $V_N$ are pieces of a graded algebra generated by five elements of degrees 1,2,3,4 and 5. The identity~\eqref{aux_0} corresponds to the unit element, while the $\zeta(5,3)$-identity above may be interpreted as the generator of degree $5$. Although we do not pursue this direction further in the present article, the numerical evidence supporting this interpretation is substantial, as illustrated by the ten examples examined here.\par
	
	The paper is organized as follows. Section~2 provides background material. In Section~3, we present six examples in which the summation formula~\eqref{aux_8} can be written explicitly, allowing coefficient extraction to yield exact and rigorous evaluations; the conjecture above is also illustrated in the first three examples. Section~4 contains four further examples in which the full formula is not written out explicitly, and we focus instead on numerical identities obtained by extracting coefficients from $G(n,0)$.
	
