\section{More Examples}
For WZ-pairs in this section, Proposition \ref{WZ_prop} will give at least a non-zero terms among $$\sum_{n\geq 0} g(n)\qquad \text{and} \qquad \lim_{n\to \infty} \sum_{k\geq 0} F(n,k).$$
There are many ways to deal with these hurdles, for example
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
	\item Au's technique \cite{au2025wilf, au2025multiple} on asymptotic analysis that finds $\lim_{n\to \infty} \sum_{k\geq 0} F(n,k)$ explicitly. 
	\item Guillera's technique \cite{guillera2025wz} of \textit{flawless WZ-pairs}, it works well for $1/\pi^k$-formulas but not so for series leading to odd zeta values (e.g. Example \ref{Ex_10}). 
\end{itemize}
We will however not address these issues in this article, consequently, some identities listed below are strictly speaking, conjectural because of our ignorance of the terms $g(n)$ and $\lim_{n\to \infty} \sum_{k\geq 0} F(n,k)$. We adopt the following convention for numeric identities:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*]
\item A single question mark above the equality sign $\stackrel{?}{=}$ indicates that the identity follows from the method of coefficient extraction and is therefore likely within reach of existing techniques.
\item A double question mark above the equality sign $\stackrel{??}{=}$ indicates that proving the identity would require substantially new ideas beyond the current work.
\end{itemize}
