\title{Laplacian Pair State Transfer on Total Graphs}

\author{ Akash Kalita and Bikash Bhattacharjya\\
Department of Mathematics\\
Indian Institute of Technology Guwahati, India\\
akash.kalita@iitg.ac.in, b.bikash@iitg.ac.in }

\date{}
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\vspace{-0.3in}

\begin{center}{\textbf{Abstract}}\end{center}
The total graph of a graph $G$, denoted $\mathcal{T}(G)$, is defined as the graph whose vertex set is the union of the vertex set of $G$ and the edge set of $G$ such that two vertices of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ are adjacent if the corresponding elements of $G$ are adjacent or incident. In this paper, we investigate Laplacian perfect pair state transfer and Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer on $\mathcal{T}(G)$, where $G$ is an $r$-regular graph. We prove that if $r>2$ and $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$, then $\mathcal{T}(G)$ fails to exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. We also prove that if $G$ is a complete graph on more than three vertices, then $\mathcal{T}(G)$ fails to exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. Further, we prove that under some mild conditions, $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer, where $G$ is an $r$-regular graph such that $r>2$ and $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. We use these conditions to obtain infinitely many total graphs exhibiting Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer.

\noindent 
\textbf{Keywords:} Total graph, Laplacian perfect pair state transfer, Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer.  
\\
\textbf{Mathematics Subject Classifications:} 05C50, 81P68  
\section{Introduction}
Let $G$ be a simple finite connected undirected graph and $L_G$ is the Laplacian matrix of $G$. Also, let $\mathbf{i} := \sqrt{-1}$ and $t$ is a real number. The \textit{transition matrix} of $G$ with respect to $L_G$, denoted $U_G(t)$, is defined as 
\begin{align*}
U_G(t) :=  \exp(-\mathbf{i}t L_G) = \sum_{j = 0}^{\infty} \frac{(-\mathbf{i}t L_G)^j}{j!}. 
\end{align*}
Let $a$ be a vertex of $G$ and $\textbf{e}_a$ denote the column vector indexed by the vertices of $G$ such that the $a$-th entry is one and zero elsewhere. If $a$ and $b$ are two distinct vertices of $G$, then the \textit{pair state} associated with $\{a, b\}$ is given by the unit vector 
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}).
\end{align*}
For convenience, we exclude the scalar $\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}$ and use $\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}$ to represent a pair state. 

A graph $G$ is said to exhibit \textit{Laplacian perfect pair state transfer} between distinct pair states ${\textbf{e}_{a}} - {\textbf{e}_{b}}$ and ${\textbf{e}_{c}} - {\textbf{e}_{d}}$ if there exists a non-zero real number $t$ such that 
$$\left|\frac{1}{2}{({\textbf{e}_{a}} - {\textbf{e}_{b}})}^TU_G(t)({\textbf{e}_{c}} - {\textbf{e}_{d}})\right| = 1.$$ 
Chen~\cite{Q. Chen Master's Thesis} and Chen, and Godsil~\cite{Q. Chen} introduced the concept of Laplacian perfect pair state transfer on graphs. Further studies about the existence of Laplacian perfect pair state transfer on graphs are done by Cao~\cite{Xiwang}, Cao and Wan~\cite{Xiwang Cao} and Luo et al.~\cite{Gaojun Luo}.



 














Laplacian perfect pair state transfer does not always exist in a graph. Therefore, Wang et al.~\cite{Vertex corona pair state transfer} introduced the concept of 
Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer in the year $2023$. A graph $G$ is said to exhibit \textit{Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer} between distinct pair states ${\textbf{e}_{a}} - {\textbf{e}_{b}}$ and ${\textbf{e}_{c}} - {\textbf{e}_{d}}$ if for every positive real number $\varepsilon$, there exists a non-zero real number $t$ such that 
$$\left|\left|\frac{1}{2}{({\textbf{e}_{a}} - {\textbf{e}_{b}})}^TU_G(t)({\textbf{e}_{c}} - {\textbf{e}_{d}})\right| - 1\right|< \varepsilon.$$ 

Cvetkovic et al.~\cite{An Introduction to the Theory of Graph Spectra} introduced the notion of total graph of a graph. The \textit{total graph} of a graph $G$, denoted $\mathcal{T}(G)$, is defined as the graph whose vertex set is the union of the vertex set of $G$ and the edge set of $G$ such that two vertices of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ are adjacent if the corresponding elements of $G$ are adjacent or incident. Liu and Wang~\cite{Total Graph 1} studied Laplacian perfect state transfer and Laplacian pretty good state transfer on $\mathcal{T}(G)$, where $G$ is an $r$-regular graph. They proved that $\mathcal{T}(G)$ does not exhibit Laplacian perfect state transfer if $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. If $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$, then Liu and Wang~\cite{Total Graph 1} provided some sufficient conditions for the existence of Laplacian pretty good state transfer on $\mathcal{T}(G)$. They also asked the question: If $G$ is $r$-regular and $r+1$ is a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$, can $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibits Laplacian perfect state transfer? In $2023$, Liu and Liu~\cite{Total Graph 2} answered the preceding question and proved that for any regular graph $G$, $\mathcal{T}(G)$ does not exhibit Laplacian perfect state transfer. Wang et al.~\cite{Vertex corona pair state transfer} studied Laplacian perfect state transfer and Laplacian pretty good state transfer on vertex coronas. Jiang et al.~\cite{Pair State Transfer Q-Graph} investigated the existence of Laplacian perfect pair state transfer and Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer on $\mathcal{Q}$- graph of a regular graph. Some of the results and ideas of this paper are influenced by that of Jiang et al.~\cite{Pair State Transfer Q-Graph}.

The subsequent sections of this paper are structured as follows. In Section~\ref{preliminary}, we introduce some basic definitions and preliminary results that we use in the rest of the sections. In Section~\ref{perfect}, we explore the existence of Laplacian perfect pair state transfer on $\mathcal{T}(G)$, where $G$ is an $r$-regular graph such that $r>2$. We prove that $\mathcal{T}(G)$ does not exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer if $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. We also prove that $\mathcal{T}(G)$ does not exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer if $G$ is the complete graph on $n$ vertices such that $n>3$. In Section~\ref{pretty good}, we explore the existence of Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer on $\mathcal{T}(G)$, where $G$ is an $r$-regular graph such that $r>2$. We prove that $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer, under some mild conditions. Using these conditions, we obtain infinite family of total graphs exhibiting Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer.  

\section{Basic Definitions and Preliminary Results}\label{preliminary}
In this section, we discuss some basic definitions and results which we use in the rest of the paper. Let $L_G$ be the Laplacian matrix of a graph $G$ and $\theta_0,\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_p$ are the distinct Laplacian eigenvalues such that $0=\theta_0<\theta_1 < \cdots < \theta_p$. Further, let  $E_{\theta_0}{(G)},E_{\theta_1}{(G)}, \ldots, E_{\theta_p}{(G)}$ be the Laplacian eigenprojector corresponding to the eigenvalues $\theta_0,\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_p$, respectively. Then the spectral decomposition of $L_G$ is given by
$$L_G = \sum_{j=0}^{p}\theta_jE_{\theta_j}{(G)}.$$
Let $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ be two distinct pair states of $G$. The \textit{Laplacian eigenvalue support} of $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$, denoted $\Phi_{ab}$, is defined as the set $\{\theta_j \colon E_{\theta_j}{(G)}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) \neq \textbf{0} \}$. The pair states $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ are called \textit{Laplacian strongly cospectral} if $E_{\theta_j}{(G)}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \pm E_{\theta_j}{(G)}(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)~~\mathrm{for}~0 \leq j \leq p$. If $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ are Laplacian strongly cospectral, then we consider the two sets $\Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ and $\Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ given by 
\begin{align*}
\Phi^{+}_{ab,cd} &= \{\theta_j \colon E_{\theta_j}{(G)}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = E_{\theta_j}{(G)}(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d) \neq \textbf{0}\}~\mathrm{and}~\\
\Phi^{-}_{ab,cd} &= \{\theta_j \colon E_{\theta_j}{(G)}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = -E_{\theta_j}{(G)}(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d) \neq \textbf{0}\}.
\end{align*}
It is clear that for Laplacian strongly cospectral pair states $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$, we have 
$$\Phi_{ab} = \Phi_{cd} = \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd} \cup \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}.$$ 
Chen and Godsil~\cite{Q. Chen} obtained necessary and sufficient conditions for the existence of Laplacian perfect pair state transfer on graphs. 
\begin{theorem}\emph{\cite{Q. Chen}}\label{Conditions for the existence of perfect pair state transfer}
Let $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ be two distinct pair states of a graph $G$. Also, let $\Phi_{ab} =\{\theta_0, \ldots, \theta_{\ell}\}$ such that $\theta_0 > \cdots > \theta_{\ell}$. Then $G$ exhibits Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$  if and only the following three conditions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] The pair states $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ are Laplacian strongly cospectral.
\item[(ii)] For $0 \leq j \leq \ell$, either $\theta_j$ is an integer or $\theta_j$ is a quadratic integer of the form $\frac{1}{2}(x + y_j \sqrt{\Delta})$, where $x, y_j$ are integers and $\Delta$ is a square-free integer strictly greater than one. If $\theta_i$ and $\theta_j$ are quadratic integers for $0 \leq i,j \leq \ell$, then $y_i$ and $y_j$ have the same parity.
\item[(iii)] Let $g=\gcd \left(\{\frac{\theta_0-\theta_j}{\sqrt{\Delta}}\}_{j=0}^{\ell}\right)$. Then

$(a)~\theta_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ if and only if $\frac{\theta_0-\theta_j}{g\sqrt{\Delta}}$ is even, and 

$(a)~\theta_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ if and only if $\frac{\theta_0-\theta_j}{g\sqrt{\Delta}}$ is odd.

Here, $\Delta = 1$ if the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are integers, otherwise $\Delta$ is a square-free integer strictly greater than one.
\end{enumerate}
If the conditions $(i)$, $(ii)$ and $(iii)$ hold, then $G$ exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer at minimum $t_0$, where $t_0$ is given by $t_0=\frac{\pi}{g \sqrt{\Delta}}$.
\end{theorem}
It is clear that the set $\Phi_{ab}$ is always non-empty for any pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $G$. The state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ is called a \textit{fixed state} if $\Phi_{ab}$ is a singleton set. Jiang et al.~\cite{Pair State Transfer Q-Graph} proved the following Lemma.
\begin{lema}\emph{\cite{Pair State Transfer Q-Graph}}\label{fixed state}
Let the graph $G$ exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. Then both of $\Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ and $\Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ are non-empty, that is, the pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ cannot be fixed.
\end{lema}
Note that the Lemma~\ref{fixed state} also follows from Godsil et al.~\cite{Real State Transfer} [see Proposition 2.3]. Now we state some number theoretic results. In the following we state a theorem on simultaneous approximation of numbers, called \textit{Kronecker's approximation theorem}.
\begin{theorem}\emph{\cite{Apostol}}\label{Kronecker approximation theorem}
Let $\alpha_1, \ldots, \alpha_{\ell}$ be arbitrary real numbers. Also, let $1, \theta_1, \ldots, \theta_{\ell}$ be real, algebraic numbers and linearly independent over $\mathbb{Q}$. Then for every positive real number $\varepsilon$, there exists integers $r,s_1, \ldots, s_{\ell}$ such that 
$$|r \theta_j - s_j -\alpha_j |<\varepsilon~~\mathrm{for}~1 \leq j \leq \ell.$$
\end{theorem}

In Section~\ref{pretty good}, the preceding theorem is our main tool to study the existence of Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer.
The next lemma tells us some sets of numbers which are linearly independent over $\mathbb{Q}$.
\begin{lema}\emph{\cite{Richards}}\label{Linearly independent corollary}
The set $\{1\} \cup \{\sqrt{\Delta} \colon \Delta~\mathrm{is~a~square~free~integer~such~that}~\Delta~\mathrm{is~stricly~greater~than~one}\}$ is linearly independent over $\mathbb{Q}$.
\end{lema}

Let $m$ and $n$ be two positive integers. Throughout this paper, $J_{m \times n}$ denotes the $m \times n$ all one matrix, $\textbf{0}_{m \times n}$ denotes the $m \times n$ zero matrix, $I_{n \times n}$ denote the $n \times n$ identity matrix, $\textbf{0}_{n}$ denotes the zero column vector with $n$ entries and $\textbf{1}_{n}$ denotes the all one column vector with $n$ entries. All the graphs considered in this paper are finite, simple, undirected and connected. Further, $K_n$ denotes the complete graph on $n$ vertices, $Q_n$ denotes the hypercube on $2^n$ vertices and $\mathrm{Cay}(\mathbb{Z}_n, S)$ denotes a circulant graph on $n$ vertices with the connection set $S$.
Let $\alpha$ and $\beta$ be complex numbers, and $\varepsilon$ is an arbitrarily small positive real number. Then we write the inequality $|\alpha - \beta| < \varepsilon$ as $\alpha \approx \beta$ and omit the dependence on $\varepsilon$.

Let $G$ be a graph with the vertex set $\{v_1, \ldots, v_n\}$ and the edge set $\{e_1, \ldots, e_m\}$. The \textit{incidence matrix} of $G$, denoted $R_G=(r_{ij})_{n \times m}$,  is defined as follows
\[r_{ij} = \left\{ \begin{array}{rl} 1 & \textrm{ if the vertex } v_i \textrm{ is incident to the edge } e_j \\
0 & \textrm{ otherwise. } \end{array}\right. \]
Liu and Wang~\cite{Total Graph 1} determined the Laplacian eigenvalues and the Laplacian eigenprojectors of the total graph of an $r$-regular graph, where $r>1$. We include it in the form of the following theorem. In what follows we consider the ordering $v_1, \ldots, v_n,e_1, \ldots, e_m$ of the vertices of the graph $\mathcal{T}(G)$. 
\begin{theorem}\emph{\cite{Total Graph 1}}\label{Total graph spectra}
Let $G$ be an $r$-regular connected graph on $n$ vertices and $m$ edges such that $r$ is strictly greater than one. Let $\theta_j~(0 \leq j \leq p)$ be the distinct Laplacian eigenvalues of $G$ such that $0=\theta_0 < \theta_1 < \cdots <\theta_p$. Also, let $\{z_1,\ldots,z_s\}$ be an orthonormal basis of the null-space of $R_G$, where
\[s = \left\{ \begin{array}{rl} m-n & \textrm{ if G is non-bipartite } \\
m-n+1 & \textrm{ if G is bipartite. } \end{array}\right. \]
Further, let
\begin{align*}
&\theta^{\pm }_j=\frac{r+2+2\theta_j \pm \sqrt{(r+2)^2-4\theta_j}}{2}~~\mathrm{and}\\
&E_{\theta^{\pm }_j}{(\mathcal{T}(G))}=\frac{1}{(2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)^2+2r-\theta_j}\begin{pmatrix}
         (2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)^2E_{\theta_j}(G) & (2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)E_{\theta_j}(G)R_G  \\
         (2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j){R^T_G}E_{\theta_j}(G)& {R^T_G}E_{\theta_j}(G)R_G  
       \end{pmatrix},
\end{align*}
where $0 \leq j \leq p$. Then we have
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] If $G$ is non-bipartite, then $\theta^{\pm }_j$ are Laplacian eigenvalues of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ with corresponding Laplacian eigenprojectors $E_{\theta^{\pm }_j}{(\mathcal{T}(G))}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$. Further, $2r+2$ is a Laplacian eigenvalue of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ with corresponding Laplacian eigenprojector given by
\begin{align*}
E_{2r+2}{(\mathcal{T}(G))}=\begin{pmatrix}
         \textbf{0}_{n \times n} & \textbf{0}_{n \times m}  \\
         \textbf{0}_{m \times n} & \sum_{i=1}^{m-n} z_iz^T_i  
       \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
\item[(ii)] If $G$ is bipartite and $X_1 \cup X_2$ is a bipartition of the vertex set of $G$, then $\theta^{\pm }_j$ are Laplacian eigenvalues of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ with corresponding Laplacian eigenprojectors $E_{\theta^{\pm }_j}{(\mathcal{T}(G))}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p-1$ and $3r$ is a Laplacian eigenvalue of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ with corresponding Laplacian eigenprojector given by
\begin{align*}
E_{3r}{(\mathcal{T}(G))}=\begin{pmatrix}
         \frac{1}{n}Q & \textbf{0}_{n \times m}  \\
         \textbf{0}_{m \times n} & \textbf{0}_{m \times m}  
       \end{pmatrix},~\mathrm{where}~
Q=\begin{pmatrix}
        J_{|X_1| \times |X_1|}  &-J_{|X_1| \times |X_2|}   \\
         -J_{|X_2| \times |X_1|} & J_{|X_2| \times |X_2|}  
       \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
Further, $2r+2$ is a Laplacian eigenvalue of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ with corresponding Laplacian eigenprojector given by 
\begin{align*}
E_{2r+2}{(\mathcal{T}(G))}=\begin{pmatrix}
         \textbf{0}_{n \times n} & \textbf{0}_{n \times m}  \\
         \textbf{0}_{m \times n} & \sum_{i=1}^{m-n+1} z_iz^T_i  
       \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}




\section{Laplacian perfect pair state transfer}\label{perfect}
Let $G$ be an $r$-regular connected graph. If $r=1$, then $G$ is the path on two vertices. It is clear that $\mathcal{T}(G)$ is the cycle on three vertices. Now Theorem 7.3 of Chen and Godsil~\cite{Q. Chen} implies that the graph $\mathcal{T}(G)$ does not exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. In what follows $G$ denotes an $r$-regular connected graph on $n$ vertices and $m$ edges such that $r>2$. Recall that if $\{v_1, \ldots, v_n\}$ is the vertex set of $G$ and $\{e_1, \ldots, e_m\}$ is the edge set of $G$, then we consider the ordering $v_1, \ldots, v_n,e_1, \ldots, e_m$ of the vertices of the graph $\mathcal{T}(G)$. Let $a$ be a vertex of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ such that $a$ is a vertex of $G$. Then the vertex state of $a$ in $\mathcal{T}(G)$, denoted $\textbf{e}_a$, is defined by the column vector
$$\begin{pmatrix}
     \textbf{e}_a \\
     \textbf{0}_m  
\end{pmatrix}.$$
Let $a$ be a vertex of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ such that $a$ is an edge of $G$. Then the vertex state of $a$ in $\mathcal{T}(G)$, denoted $\textbf{e}_a$, is defined by the column vector
$$\begin{pmatrix}
     \textbf{0}_n \\
     \textbf{e}_a 
\end{pmatrix}.$$
Using some ideas from Jiang et al~\cite{Pair State Transfer Q-Graph}, we prove the following lemma.
\begin{lema}\label{perfect state transfer lemma non-bipartite}
Let $G$ be a non-bipartite graph. If $a$ and $b$ are vertices of $G$ or $a$ and $b$ are edges of $G$, then the following two conditions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$.
\item[(ii)] Let $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ be Laplacian strongly cospectral pair states of $\mathcal{T}(G)$. Then 
$\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$. Further, $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$.
\end{enumerate} 
\end{lema}
\begin{proof}
$(i)$ If $a$ and $b$ are vertices of $G$, then Theorem~\ref{Total graph spectra} implies that 
\begin{equation}\label{x_1}
E_{\theta^{\pm }_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \frac{1}{(2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)^2+2r-\theta_j}\begin{pmatrix}
         (2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)^2E_{\theta_j}(G)(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b)  \\
         (2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j){R^T_G}E_{\theta_j}(G)(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b)  
       \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}  
If possible, let $2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j = 0$ for some $j$ such that $0 \leq j \leq p$. Then it follows that $\theta_j = 2r$, which is a contradiction. Thus, we have $2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j \neq 0$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$. Now $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ if and only if $E_{\theta^{+}_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) \neq \textbf{0}$ if and only if $E_{\theta_j}(G)(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) \neq \textbf{0}$ if and only if $E_{\theta^{-}_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) \neq \textbf{0}$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$. 

If $a$ and $b$ are edges of $G$, then Theorem~\ref{Total graph spectra} implies that  
\begin{equation}\label{y_1}
E_{\theta^{\pm }_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \frac{1}{(2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)^2+2r-\theta_j}\begin{pmatrix}
         (2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)E_{\theta_j}(G)R_G(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b)  \\
         {R^T_G}E_{\theta_j}(G)R_G (\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b)
       \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Now $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ if and only if $E_{\theta^{+}_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) \neq \textbf{0}$ if and only if $E_{\theta_j}(G)R_G(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) \neq \textbf{0}$ if and only if 
$E_{\theta^{-}_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) \neq \textbf{0}$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$.

$(ii)$ If $a$ and $b$ are vertices of $G$ such that the pair states $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ are Laplacian strongly cospectral of $\mathcal{T}(G)$, then
\begin{equation}\label{y_2}
E_{\theta^{\pm }_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \pm E_{\theta^{\pm }_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)~~~\mathrm{for}~0 \leq j \leq p.
\end{equation}
Since $E_{\theta_0}(G)(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b)=0$ and $\theta^{+}_0=r+2$, from Equation~(\ref{x_1}) and Equation~(\ref{y_2}) we obtain that  
\begin{equation}\label{x_2}
E_{r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \pm E_{r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)=\textbf{0}.
\end{equation}
From Theorem~\ref{Total graph spectra}, we have 
\begin{equation}\label{x_3}
E_{r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G)) = \frac{1}{r^2+2r}\begin{pmatrix}
         \frac{r^2}{n} J_{n\times n} &  \frac{-2r}{n} J_{n \times m}\\
          \frac{-2r}{n}J_{m \times n} &  \frac{4}{n} J_{m \times m}
       \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}
Now Equation~(\ref{x_2}) and Equation~(\ref{x_3}) together imply that either $c,d$ are vertices of $G$ or $c,d$ are edges of $G$. Suppose that $c$ and $d$ are edges of the graph 
$G$. Then   
\begin{equation}\label{x_4}
E_{\theta^{\pm }_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d) = \frac{1}{(2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)^2+2r-\theta_j}\begin{pmatrix}
         (2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)E_{\theta_j}(G)R_G(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)  \\
         {R^T_G}E_{\theta_j}(G)R_G(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)  
       \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}      
Consider $j$ such that $0 \leq j \leq p$. If $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd},~\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ or $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd},~\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$, then $\theta^{+}_j + \theta^{-}_j = 2\theta_j + 4$. This implies that $r=2$, which is a contradiction. If $\theta^{+}_j,\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ or $\theta^{+}_j, \theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$, then $\theta^{+}_j = \theta^{-}_j$. This implies $(r+2)^2-4\theta_j=0$. This is a contradiction as $0 < (r-2)^2 < (r+2)^2-4\theta_j$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$. Thus $c$ and $d$ are vertices of $G$. This implies 
\begin{equation}\label{x_5}
E_{\theta^{\pm }_j}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d) = \frac{1}{(2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)^2+2r-\theta_j}\begin{pmatrix}
         (2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j)^2E_{\theta_j}(G)(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)  \\
         (2+\theta_j-\theta^{\pm }_j){R^T_G}E_{\theta_j}(G)(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)  
       \end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}          
Now $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ if and only if $E_{\theta_j}(G)(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = E_{\theta_j}(G)(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$. Similarly, it can be proved that $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$. 

If $a$ and $b$ are edges of $G$ such that $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ are Laplacian strongly cospectral, then following the same argument as in the preceding part, we obtain that $c$ and $d$ are also edges of $G$. Now the rest of the proof follows is similar to that of the preceding part.
\end{proof}

Using the same technique as in Lemma~\ref{perfect state transfer lemma non-bipartite}, we prove the following lemma. The details are omitted.
\begin{lema}\label{perfect state transfer lemma bipartite}
Let $G$ be a bipartite graph. If $a$ and $b$ are vertices of $G$ or $a$ and $b$ are edges of $G$, then the following two conditions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p-1$.
\item[(ii)] Let $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ be Laplacian strongly cospectral pair states of $\mathcal{T}(G)$. Then 
$\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p-1$. Further, $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ if and only if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p-1$.
\end{enumerate} 
\end{lema}

Now we use Lemma~\ref{perfect state transfer lemma non-bipartite} and Lemma~\ref{perfect state transfer lemma bipartite} to prove the next four lemmas.
\begin{lema}\label{non-existence of PST 1st Lemma}
Let $a$ and $b$ be two vertices of a non-bipartite graph $G$. Also, let $r+1$ not be a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. Then the pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer.  
\end{lema}
\begin{proof}
Let the graph $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between the pair states $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. By Theorem~\ref{Conditions for the existence of perfect pair state transfer}, either all the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are integers or all the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are quadratic integers. Since $a$ and $b$ are two vertices of $G$, we have 
$$E_{\theta^{\pm }_0}{(\mathcal{T}(G))}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = E_{2r+2}{(\mathcal{T}(G))}(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d) = \textbf{0}.$$
This implies that $\Phi_{ab} \subseteq \{\theta^{\pm }_j \colon 1 \leq j \leq p\}$. Consider the case that the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are integers. From Lemma~\ref{perfect state transfer lemma non-bipartite}, we obtain that either $\theta^{\pm }_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ or $\theta^{\pm }_j \notin \Phi_{ab}$ for $ 1 \leq j \leq p$. Suppose that there exists $j$ with $1 \leq j \leq p$ and $\theta^{\pm }_j \in \Phi_{ab}$. Then $\theta_j$ is an integer and $(r+2)^2-4\theta_j$ is a perfect square. Since $0 < \theta_j < 2r$, we have $\sqrt{(r+2)^2-4\theta_j} \in \{r-1,r,r+1\}$. If $\sqrt{(r+2)^2-4\theta_j} = r-1$ or $\sqrt{(r+2)^2-4\theta_j} = r+1$, then $\theta^{\pm }_j \notin \mathbb{Z}$, which is a contradiction. If $\sqrt{(r+2)^2-4\theta_j} = r$, then $\theta_j = r+1$, which is again a contradiction. Thus we prove that $\theta^{\pm }_j \notin \Phi_{ab}$ for $1 \leq j \leq p$. This implies that $\Phi_{ab}$ is an empty set, which is a contradiction as $\Phi_{ab}$ can never be an empty set.

Now we consider that the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are quadratic integers. Let $\theta^{\pm }_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ for some $j$ such that $1 \leq j \leq p$. Then Theorem~\ref{Conditions for the existence of perfect pair state transfer} yields that $\theta^{\pm }_j=\frac{1}{2}(x+y_{\pm}\sqrt{\Delta})$, where $\Delta$ is a square-free integer such that $\Delta>1$, $x,y_{\pm} \in \mathbb{Z}$ and $y_{+}$, and $y_{-}$ have the same parity. Now we have 
$$x + \frac{1}{2}(y_{+} + y_{-})\sqrt{\Delta} = \theta^{+}_j + \theta^{-}_j = r+2+2\theta_j.$$
This implies that
\begin{equation}\label{x_7}
4\theta_j = 2(x - r - 2) + (y_{+} + y_{-})\sqrt{\Delta}.
\end{equation}
From Theorem~\ref{Total graph spectra} and Equation~(\ref{x_7}), we obtain that 
\begin{equation}\label{x_8}
4\theta^{+}_j\theta^{-}_j = (x-r-2)(x+r+2)+\frac{(y_{+}+y_{-})^2}{4}\Delta + (x-r+2)(y_{+}+y_{-})\sqrt{\Delta}.
\end{equation}
Also, we have
\begin{equation}\label{x_9}
4\theta^{+}_j\theta^{-}_j = x^2 + y_{+}y_{-}\Delta + x(y_{+}+y_{-})\sqrt{\Delta}.
\end{equation}
As $\sqrt{\Delta}$ is an irrational number, Equation~(\ref{x_8}) and Equation~(\ref{x_9}) yields that 
$$(x-r+2)(y_{+}+y_{-}) = x(y_{+}+y_{-}).$$
If $y_{+}+y_{-} \neq 0$, then $r=2$, a contradiction. If $y_{+}+y_{-} = 0$, then $\theta_j=\frac{x - r - 2}{2}$. This implies that 
$$\Phi_{ab} = \left\{\left(\frac{x - r - 2}{2}\right)^{-},\left(\frac{x - r - 2}{2}\right)^{+}\right\}.$$
By Lemma~\ref{perfect state transfer lemma non-bipartite}, we have 
$$\left(\frac{x - r - 2}{2}\right)^{-},\left(\frac{x - r - 2}{2}\right)^{+} \in \Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}~~\mathrm{or}~~\left(\frac{x - r - 2}{2}\right)^{-},\left(\frac{x - r - 2}{2}\right)^{+} \in \Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}.$$
This implies that either $\Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ is empty or $\Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ is empty. This contradicts Theorem~\ref{fixed state}. Therefore, $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer.
\end{proof}


\begin{lema}\label{non-existence of PST 2nd Lemma}
Let $a$ and $b$ be two vertices of a bipartite graph $G$. Also, let $r+1$ not be a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. Then the pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer.   
\end{lema}
\begin{proof}
Let $X_1 \cup X_2$ is a bipartition of the vertex set of $G$. Let $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. We have $\Phi_{ab} \subseteq \{\theta^{\pm }_j \colon 1 \leq j \leq p-1\} \cup \{3r\}$. Now consider the following two cases.

\noindent \textbf{Case 1.} If $a, b \in X_1$ or $a, b \in X_2$, then $3r \notin \Phi_{ab}$. Therefore implies that $\Phi_{ab} \subseteq \{\theta^{\pm }_j \colon 1 \leq j \leq p-1\}$. If the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are integers, then following the procedure of Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 1st Lemma}, we obtain that $\theta^{\pm }_j \notin \Phi_{ab}$ for $1 \leq j \leq p-1$. This implies $\Phi_{ab}$ is empty, which is a contradiction. If the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are quadratic integers, then following the procedure and the notations of Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 1st Lemma}, we obtain that
$$\Phi_{ab} = \left\{\left(\frac{x - r - 2}{2}\right)^{-},\left(\frac{x - r - 2}{2}\right)^{+}\right\}.$$
This is again a contradiction.

\noindent \textbf{Case 2.} If $a \in X_1,~b \in X_2$ or $b \in X_1,~a \in X_2$, then $3r \in \Phi_{ab}$. Since the pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ involves in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer, Theorem~\ref{Conditions for the existence of perfect pair state transfer} implies that all the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are integers. It can be proved that $\theta^{\pm }_j \notin \Phi_{ab}$ for $1 \leq j \leq p-1$. This implies $\Phi_{ab} = \{3r\}$. This contradicts Theorem~\ref{fixed state}. Therefore, $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer.
\end{proof}

\begin{lema}\label{non-existence of PST 3rd Lemma}
Let $a$ and $b$ be two edges of a non-bipartite graph $G$. Also, let $r+1$ not be a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. Then the pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer.  
\end{lema}
\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. Since $a$ and $b$ are two edges of $G$, we have 
$$E_{\theta^{\pm }_0}{(\mathcal{T}(G))}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \textbf{0}.$$
This implies that $\Phi_{ab} \subseteq \{\theta^{\pm }_j \colon 1 \leq j \leq p\} \cup \{2r+2\}$. If all the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are integers, then following the method of Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 1st Lemma}, we find that $\Phi_{ab} = \{2r+2\}$. This is a contradiction.  If all the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are quadratic integers, then $\Phi_{ab} \subseteq \{\theta^{\pm }_j \colon 1 \leq j \leq p\}$. Now the rest of the proof is similar to that of Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 1st Lemma}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lema}\label{non-existence of PST 4th Lemma}
Let $a$ and $b$ be two edges of a bipartite graph $G$. Also, let $r+1$ not be a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. Then the pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer.  
\end{lema}
\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. Since $a$ and $b$ are two edges of $G$, we have $\theta^{-}_0,\theta^{+}_0,3r \notin \Phi_{ab}$. Therefore, we have $\Phi_{ab} \subseteq \{\theta^{\pm }_j \colon 1 \leq j \leq p-1\} \cup \{2r+2\}$. The rest of the proof follows similarly to that of Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 1st Lemma}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lema}\label{non-existence of PST 5th Lemma}
Let $a$ be a vertex and $b$ be an edge of a non-bipartite graph $G$. Also, let $r+1$ not be a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. Then the pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. 
\end{lema}
\begin{proof}
Let the graph $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between the pair states $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. Since $a$ is a vertex of $G$ and $b$ is an edge of $G$, we obtain that
$$E_{\theta^{+}_0}(\mathcal{T}(G))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) \neq \textbf{0}.$$
This implies that $\theta^{+}_0 \in \Phi_{ab}$. Since $\theta^{+}_0=r+2$ and $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ involves in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer, Theorem~\ref{Conditions for the existence of perfect pair state transfer} yields that all the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are integers. Also, Theorem~\ref{fixed state} implies that $\Phi_{ab}$ contains at least two distinct eigenvalues. Let $\theta^{+}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ such that $\theta^{+}_j \neq r + 2$ and $1 \leq j \leq p$. From Theorem~\ref{Total graph spectra}, we recall that 
\begin{equation}\label{a_1}
\theta^{\pm }_j=\frac{r+2+2\theta_j \pm \sqrt{(r+2)^2-4\theta_j}}{2}.
\end{equation}
Then it follows that $\theta_j$ satisfies the following quadratic equation
\begin{equation}\label{a_2}
t^2 + (r - 2\theta^{+}_j + 3)t + \theta^{+}_j(\theta^{+}_j - r - 2) = 0.
\end{equation}
Equation~(\ref{a_2}) yields that $\theta_j$ is an integer. Also, Equation~(\ref{a_1}) yields that $\theta^{+}_j + \theta^{-}_j = r+2+2 \theta_j$. This implies that $\theta^{-}_j$ is an integer. Similarly, it can be proved that if $\theta^{-}_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ such that $\theta^{-}_j \neq r + 2$ and $1 \leq j \leq p$, then $\theta^{+}_j$ is an integer. Now following the proof of Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 1st Lemma}, we arrive at a contradiction. This implies $\Phi_{ab} = \{r+2,2r+2\}$. Then we have
$$\left(E_{r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))+E_{2r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))\right)(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b)=\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b.$$
This implies 
$$E_{r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))_{a,a}-E_{r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))_{a,b}+E_{2r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))_{a,a}-E_{2r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))_{a,b} = 1.$$
Now it follows that $n =1$, which is a contradiction. Therefore, the pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. 
\end{proof}

\begin{lema}\label{non-existence of PST 6th Lemma}
Let $a$ be a vertex and $b$ be an edge of a bipartite graph $G$. Also, let $r+1$ not be a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. Then the pair state $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. 
\end{lema}
\begin{proof}
Let $X_1 \cup X_2$ be a bipartition of the vertex set of $G$. Also, let $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. Since $a$ is a vertex and $b$ is an edge of $G$, we have  $r+2,3r \in \Phi_{ab}$. This implies that all the eigenvalues of $\Phi_{ab}$ are integers. Then following the proof of Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 5th Lemma}, we obtain that $\Phi_{ab} \subseteq \{r+2,2r+2,3r\}$. If $2r+2 \notin \Phi_{ab}$, then $\Phi_{ab} = \{r+2,3r\}$. Now 
$$\left(E_{r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))+E_{3r}(\mathcal{T}(G))\right)(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b)=\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b.$$
This implies that $\frac{1}{n}=0$, which is a contradiction. If $2r+2 \in \Phi_{ab}$, then $\Phi_{ab} = \{r+2,2r+2,3r\}$. Now
$$\left(E_{r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))+E_{2r+2}(\mathcal{T}(G))+E_{3r}(\mathcal{T}(G))\right)(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b)=\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b.$$
This implies that $\frac{1}{n}=0$, which is a contradiction. Therefore, $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ of $\mathcal{T}(G)$ cannot involved in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer.  
\end{proof}

On combining Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 1st Lemma} to Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 6th Lemma}, we obtain the following theorem.
\begin{theorem}\label{non-existence of PST main result}
Let $G$ be an $r$-regular connected graph such that $r>2$ and $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$. Then the total graph of $G$ does not exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. 
\end{theorem}

Now we study Laplacian perfect pair state transfer on total graphs of complete graph. Let $n$ be an integer such that $n > 3,~\theta_0 = 0$ and $\theta_1 = n$. Then $\theta_0$ and $\theta_1$ are the Laplacian eigenvalues of $K_n$ with corresponding Laplacian eigenprojectors given by 
$$E_{\theta_0}(K_n) = \frac{1}{n}J_{n \times n}~~\mathrm{and}~~E_{\theta_1}(K_n) = I_{n \times n} - \frac{1}{n}J_{n \times n}.$$
Let $R_G$ be the incidence matrix of $K_n$ and $\{z_1,\ldots,z_{m-n}\}$ is an orthonormal basis of the null-space of $R_G$, where $m=\frac{n^2-n}{2}$. Applying Theorem~\ref{Total graph spectra}, we obtain the Laplacian eigenvalues of $\mathcal{T}(K_n)$ as follows
$$\theta_0^{-}=0,~\theta_0^{+}=n+1,~\theta_1^{-}=n+1,~\theta_1^{+}=2n ~~\mathrm{and}~~2n.$$ Further, the corresponding Laplacian eigenprojectors are 
\begin{align*}
&E_{\theta_0^{-}}{(\mathcal{T}(K_n))} = \frac{2}{n^2+n}J_{(n+m) \times (n+m)},\\
&E_{\theta_0^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n)) = \frac{1}{n^2-1}\begin{pmatrix}
         \frac{n^2-2n+1}{n} J_{n\times n} &  \frac{-2n+2}{n} J_{n \times m}\\
          \frac{-2n+2}{n}J_{m \times n} &  \frac{4}{n} J_{m \times m}
       \end{pmatrix},\\
&E_{\theta_1^{-}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n)) = \frac{1}{n-1}\begin{pmatrix}
         I_{n \times n} - \frac{1}{n}J_{n \times n}  &  R_G-\frac{2}{n} J_{n \times m}\\
           R^T_G-\frac{2}{n} J_{m \times n} & R^T_G R_G - \frac{4}{n}J_{m \times m} 
       \end{pmatrix},\\
&E_{\theta_1^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n)) = \frac{1}{n^2 - 3n + 2}\begin{pmatrix}
         {(2-n)}^2I_{n \times n} - \frac{1}{n}J_{n \times n}  &  (2-n)R_G-\frac{2}{n} J_{n \times m}\\
            (2-n)R^T_G-\frac{2}{n} J_{m \times n} & R^T_G R_G - \frac{4}{n}J_{m \times m} 
       \end{pmatrix}~\mathrm{and}\\
&E_{2n}{(\mathcal{T}(K_n))}=\begin{pmatrix}
         \textbf{0}_{n \times n} & \textbf{0}_{n \times m}  \\
         \textbf{0}_{m \times n} & \sum_{i=1}^{m-n} z_iz^T_i  
       \end{pmatrix}.       
\end{align*}

The following theorem tells that if a complete graph contains more than three vertices, then the corresponding total graph does not exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. 
\begin{theorem}\label{Total graphs of complete graphs}
Let $n$ be an integer such that $n > 3$. Then the graph $\mathcal{T}(K_n)$ does not exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. 
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Suppose that the graph $\mathcal{T}(K_n)$ exhibits Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between the pair states $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. Consider the following three cases.

\noindent \textbf{Case 1.} Let $a$ and $b$ be vertices of $K_n$. Then we obtain that
$$E_{\theta_0^{-}}{(\mathcal{T}(K_n))}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = E_{\theta_0^{+}}{(\mathcal{T}(K_n))}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = E_{2n}{(\mathcal{T}(K_n))}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b)= \textbf{0}.$$
This implies $\Phi_{ab} = \{{\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+}\}$. Now Lemma~\ref{perfect state transfer lemma non-bipartite} yields that either ${\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+},\Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ or ${\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+},\Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$. Then either $\Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ is empty or $\Phi^{-}_{ab,cd}$ is empty. This is a contradiction by Lemma~\ref{fixed state}.

\noindent \textbf{Case 2.} Let $a$ and $b$ be edges of $K_n$. Then we have
$$E_{\theta_0^{-}}{(\mathcal{T}(K_n))}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = E_{\theta_0^{+}}{(\mathcal{T}(K_n))}(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \textbf{0}.$$
This implies that $\Phi_{ab} \subseteq \{{\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+}, 2n\}$. By Lemma~\ref{perfect state transfer lemma non-bipartite}, we have ${\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+} \in \Phi_{ab}$ or ${\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+} \notin \Phi_{ab}$. If ${\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+} \notin \Phi_{ab}$, then $\Phi_{ab} = \{2n\}$. This is a contradiction as $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ involves in Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. Therefore, we assume that ${\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+} \in \Phi_{ab}$. If $2n \notin \Phi_{ab}$, then $\Phi_{ab} = \{{\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+}\}$, which is not possible. If $2n \in \Phi_{ab}$, then $\Phi_{ab} = \{{\theta_1}^{-},{\theta_1}^{+},2n\}$. Now Theorem~\ref{Conditions for the existence of perfect pair state transfer} yields that $\Phi^{+}_{ab,cd}$ is empty. This is a contradiction. 

\noindent \textbf{Case 3.} Let $a$ be a vertex of $K_n$ and $b$ be an edge of $K_n$. Since $\mathcal{T}(K_n)$ exhibits Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$, Theorem~\ref{Conditions for the existence of perfect pair state transfer} yields that $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ are Laplacian strongly cospectral. This implies 
\begin{align}
&E_{{\theta_0}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \pm E_{{\theta_0}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)~\mathrm{and}~\label{t_1}\\
&E_{{\theta_1}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \pm E_{{\theta_1}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)\label{t_2}.
\end{align}
From Equation~(\ref{t_1}), it follows that either $c$ is a vertex of $K_n$ and $d$ is an edge of $K_n$; or $d$ is a vertex of $K_n$ and $c$ is an edge of $K_n$. It is enough to consider that $c$ is a vertex of $K_n$ and $d$ is an edge of $K_n$. Now
\begin{align*}
&E_{{\theta_1}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = \frac{1}{n^2-3n+2}\begin{pmatrix}
         (2-n)^2\textbf{e}_a-(2-n)R_G\textbf{e}_b + \frac{1}{n}\textbf{1}_n \\
         (2-n)R^T_G\textbf{e}_a - R^T_G R_G\textbf{e}_b + \frac{2}{n}\textbf{1}_m  
       \end{pmatrix}~\mathrm{and}~\\
&E_{{\theta_1}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d) = \frac{1}{n^2-3n+2}\begin{pmatrix}
         (2-n)^2\textbf{e}_c-(2-n)R_G\textbf{e}_d + \frac{1}{n}\textbf{1}_n \\
         (2-n)R^T_G\textbf{e}_c - R^T_G R_G\textbf{e}_d + \frac{2}{n}\textbf{1}_m  
       \end{pmatrix}.
\end{align*}
If $E_{{\theta_1}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = E_{{\theta_1}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)$, then $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_c = \frac{1}{2-n}R_G(\textbf{e}_b - \textbf{e}_d)$. This is not possible. If  
$E_{{\theta_1}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b) = -E_{{\theta_1}^{+}}(\mathcal{T}(K_n))(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d)$, then $(2-n)^2(\textbf{e}_a + \textbf{e}_c)+\frac{2}{n}\textbf{1}_n = (2-n)R_G(\textbf{e}_b + \textbf{e}_d)$, which is not possible. Therefore, the graph $\mathcal{T}(K_n)$ does not exhibit Laplacian perfect pair state transfer. This completes the proof.
\end{proof} 



 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
  


























\section{Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer}\label{pretty good}
In this section, we prove that there is Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer on total graphs of regular connected graphs, under some mild conditions. Recall that $G$ denotes an $r$-regular connected graph, where $r > 2$ and $\theta_0,\theta_1, \ldots, \theta_p$ are the distinct Laplacian eigenvalues of $G$ such that $0=\theta_0 < \theta_1 < \cdots < \theta_p$. Let $\Delta_j = \sqrt{(r + 2)^2 - 4 \theta_j}$, where $0 \leq j \leq p$ if $G$ is non-bipartite and $0 \leq j \leq p-1$ if $G$ is bipartite. Now we state a theorem proved by Liu and Wang~\cite{Total Graph 1}.

\begin{theorem}\emph{\cite{Total Graph 1}}\label{Total graph entry vertex state}
Let $a$ and $b$ be vertices of the graph $G$. Then the following two conditions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $G$ is a non-bipartite graph, then
\begin{align*}
U_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)_{a,b} =\sum_{j=0}^{p}\exp(-\textbf{i}t((r + 2\theta_j + 2)/2))E_{\theta_j}(G)_{a,b}\left(\cos\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) + \textbf{i}\frac{2-r}{\Delta_j}\sin\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right)\right).
\end{align*} 

\item[(b)] If $G$ is a bipartite graph, then
\begin{align*}
&U_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)_{a,b}
=\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}\exp(-\textbf{i}t((r+ 2\theta_j + 2)/2))E_{\theta_j}(G)_{a,b}\left(\cos\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) + \textbf{i}\frac{2-r}{\Delta_j}\sin\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right)\right)\\
&+ \exp(-\textbf{i}3tr)E_{2r}(G)_{a,b}.
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

Now we use Theorem~\ref{Total graph entry vertex state} to prove the following lemma. The proof is straightforward and hence the details are omitted.
\begin{lema}\label{Total graph entry pair state}
Let $a,b,c$ and $d$ be vertices of the graph $G$. Then the following two conditions hold.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] If $G$ is a non-bipartite graph, then
\begin{align*}
&\frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b\right)}^TU_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)\left(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d\right) =
\frac{1}{2}\sum_{j=0}^{p}
\exp(-\textbf{i}t((r+2\theta_j+2)/2))
{\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TE_{\theta_j}(G)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right)\\
&\times \left(\cos\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) + \textbf{i}\frac{2-r}{\Delta_j}\sin\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right)\right).
\end{align*}

\item[(b)] If $G$ is a bipartite graph, then
\begin{align*}
&\frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b\right)}^TU_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)\left(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d\right) =
\frac{1}{2}\sum_{j=0}^{p-1}
\exp(-\textbf{i}t((r+2\theta_j+2)/2))
{\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TE_{\theta_j}(G)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right)\\
&\times\left(\cos\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) + \textbf{i}\frac{2-r}{\Delta_j}\sin\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right)\right) + \frac{1}{2} \exp(-\textbf{i}3tr){\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TE_{2r}(G)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right).
\end{align*}
\end{enumerate}
\end{lema}

The following theorem provides sufficient conditions for the existence of Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer on total graph of a regular non-bipartite graph.
\begin{theorem}\label{First theorem PGST}
Let $G$ be a non-bipartite graph and $G$ exhibits Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ at $\frac{\pi}{2}$. If $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$ and $\frac{r+2}{4}$ is an integer, then $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$.
\end{theorem}    
\begin{proof}
Recall that $\Phi_{ab}$ denotes the Laplacian eigenvalue support of $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ in $G$. It is obvious that $0 \notin \Phi_{ab}$. Since $G$ exhibits perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ at $\frac{\pi}{2}$, Theorem~\ref{Conditions for the existence of perfect pair state transfer} yields that all the eigenvalues in $\Phi_{ab}$ are integers. As $\Delta_j = \sqrt{(r + 2)^2 - 4 \theta_j}$ for $0 \leq j \leq p$, therefore $\Delta^2_j$ is an integer for all $\theta_j \in \Phi_{ab}$. Let $y_j$ be a square free integer such that $\Delta_j = x_j \sqrt{y_j}$ for some integer $x_j$ and $\theta_j \in \Phi_{ab}$. As $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$, from the proof of Lemma~\ref{non-existence of PST 1st Lemma} we find that $\Delta_j$ is irrational for all $\theta_j \in \Phi_{ab}$. This implies that $y_j > 1$ for all $\theta_j \in \Phi_{ab}$. Then Lemma~\ref{Linearly independent corollary} yields that the set $\{1\} \cup \{\sqrt{y_j} \colon \theta_j \in \Phi_{ab}\}$ is linearly independent over $\mathbb{Q}$. Now applying Kronecker's approximation theorem we obtain that there exists $\ell, s_j \in \mathbb{Z}$, where $\theta_j \in \Phi_{ab}$ such that
$$\ell \sqrt{y_j} - s_j \approx -\frac{\sqrt{y_j}}{8}~~\mathrm{for~all}~\theta_j \in  \Phi_{ab}.$$
This implies that
$$\left(4 \ell + \frac{1}{2}\right)\Delta_j \approx 4x_j s_j~~\mathrm{for~all}~\theta_j \in  \Phi_{ab}.$$
Let $t=(4\ell+\frac{1}{2})\pi$. Since the functions $\cos$ and $\sin$ are uniformly continuous on $\mathbb{R}$, we have 
$$\cos\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) \approx 1~~\mathrm{and}~~\sin\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) \approx 0~~\mathrm{for}~0 \leq j \leq p~\mathrm{and}~\theta_j \in  \Phi_{ab}.$$   
From part $(a)$ of Lemma~\ref{Total graph entry pair state}, we obtain that
\begin{align*}
&\frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b\right)}^TU_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)\left(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d\right) = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{\theta_j \in \Phi_{ab}}\exp(-\textbf{i}t((r + 2\theta_j + 2)/2)){\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TE_{\theta_j}(G)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right)\\
&\times \left(\cos\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) + \textbf{i}\frac{2 - r}{\Delta_j}\sin\left(\frac{\Delta_j}{2}\right)\right)\\
& \approx \pm \frac{1}{2}\sum_{\theta_j \in  \Phi_{ab}}\exp(-\textbf{i}\theta_j(\pi/2)){\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TE_{\theta_j}(G)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right) = \pm \frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^T U_G(\pi/2)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right).
\end{align*}
Since $G$ exhibits Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between ${\textbf{e}_{a}} - {\textbf{e}_{b}}$ and ${\textbf{e}_{c}} - {\textbf{e}_{d}}$ at $\frac{\pi}{2}$, we have 
$$\left|\frac{1}{2}{({\textbf{e}_{a}} - {\textbf{e}_{b}})}^TU_G(\pi/2)({\textbf{e}_{c}} - {\textbf{e}_{d}})\right| = 1.$$
It follows that  
$$\left|\frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b\right)}^TU_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)\left(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d\right)\right| \approx 1.$$
Now it is clear that the graph $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer between the pair states ${\textbf{e}_{a}} - {\textbf{e}_{b}}$ and ${\textbf{e}_{c}} - {\textbf{e}_{d}}$.
\end{proof}

In the following, we illustrate Theorem~\ref{First theorem PGST} with the help of an example.
\begin{ex}
Consider the cocktail party graph $\overline{\cup_m K_2}$, where $m = 4m_1 + 2$ for some positive integer $m_1$. It is a $2m-2$ regular non-bipartite graph and the distinct Laplacian eigenvalues are $0,2m-2,2m$. From Coutinho et al.~\cite{Coutinho} [see Corollary 5.1 ] it follows that $\overline{\cup_m K_2}$ exhibits Laplacian perfect state transfer at $\frac{\pi}{2}$. Since $\overline{\cup_m K_2}=\mathrm{Cay}(\mathbb{Z}_{2m}, \mathbb{Z}_{2m} \setminus \{0,m\})$, Lemma 3.1 of Luo et al.~\cite{Gaojun Luo} implies that $\overline{\cup_m K_2}$ exhibits Laplacian perfect pair state transfer at $\frac{\pi}{2}$. Also, $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $\overline{\cup_m K_2}$ and $\frac{r+2}{4}$ is an integer. Now Theorem~\ref{First theorem 
PGST} yields that the total graph of $\overline{\cup_m K_2}$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer. 
\end{ex}

The next theorem provides sufficient conditions for the existence of Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer on total graph of a regular bipartite graph.
\begin{theorem}\label{Second theorem PGST}
Let $G$ be a bipartite graph and $X_1 \cup X_2$ is a bipartition of the vertex set of $G$. Also, let $G$ exhibits Laplacian perfect pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$ at $\frac{\pi}{2}$ and $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue of $G$.   
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Suppose that $a, b \in X_1$ or $a, b \in X_2$. If $\frac{r+2}{4}$ is an integer, then $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$.
\item[(b)] Suppose that $a \in X_1,~b \in X_2$ or $b \in X_1,~a \in X_2$. If $\frac{3r}{2}$ and $\frac{5r+2}{4}$ are integers of the same parities, then $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. 
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Following the notations and the proof of Theorem~\ref{First theorem PGST}, we obtain that
$$\cos\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) \approx 1~~\mathrm{and}~~\sin\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) \approx 0,~~\mathrm{where}~t=(4\ell+\frac{1}{2})\pi,~\theta_j \in  \Phi_{ab}~~\mathrm{and}~~0 \leq j \leq p-1.$$   

\noindent $(a)$ If $a, b \in X_1$ or $a, b \in X_2$, then $2r$ does not belong to $\Phi_{ab}$. From part $(b)$ of Lemma~\ref{Total graph entry pair state}, we have 
\begin{align*}
&\frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b\right)}^TU_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)\left(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d\right) = \frac{1}{2}\sum_{\theta_j \in \Phi_{ab}}\exp(-\textbf{i}t((r + 2\theta_j + 2)/2)){\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TE_{\theta_j}(G)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right)\\
&\times \left(\cos\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) + \textbf{i}\frac{2 - r}{\Delta_j}\sin\left(\frac{\Delta_j}{2}\right)\right)
\approx \pm \frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^T U_G(\pi/2)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right).
\end{align*}
This implies  
$$\left|\frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b\right)}^TU_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)\left(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d\right)\right| \approx 1.$$
Therefore, $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$.

\noindent $(b)$ If $a \in X_1,~b \in X_2$ or $b \in X_1,~a \in X_2$, then $2r$ belongs to $\Phi_{ab}$. Now we have 
\begin{align*}
&\frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b\right)}^TU_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)\left(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d\right)
=
\frac{1}{2} \sum_{\theta_j \in \Phi_{ab} \setminus \{2r\}}
\exp(-\textbf{i}t((r+2\theta_j+2)/2))
{\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TE_{\theta_j}(G)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right)\\
&\times\left(\cos\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right) + \textbf{i}\frac{2-r}{\Delta_j}\sin\left(\frac{\Delta_j t}{2}\right)\right) + \frac{1}{2} \exp(-3\textbf{i}tr){\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TE_{2r}(G)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right)\\
&\approx \frac{1}{2} \sum_{\theta_j \in \Phi_{ab}}
\exp(-\textbf{i}t((r+2\theta_j+2)/2))
{\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TE_{\theta_j}(G)\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right)
=-\frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_{a} - \textbf{e}_{b}\right)}^TU_G(\pi /2)     {\left(\textbf{e}_{c} - \textbf{e}_{d}\right)}.
\end{align*}
This implies  
$$\left|\frac{1}{2}{\left(\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b\right)}^TU_{\mathcal{T}(G)}(t)\left(\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d\right)\right| \approx 1.$$
Therefore, $\mathcal{T}(G)$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer between $\textbf{e}_a - \textbf{e}_b$ and $\textbf{e}_c - \textbf{e}_d$. 
\end{proof}

The following example illustrates Theorem~\ref{Second theorem PGST}.
\begin{ex}
Consider the hypercube $Q_r$, where $r=4m_1+2$ and $m_1$ is an even integer. Since $Q_r$ exhibits Laplacian perfect state transfer at $\frac{\pi}{2}$, it exhibits Laplacian perfect pair state transfer at $\frac{\pi}{2}$. Clearly, $Q_r$ is a $r$-regular bipartite graph and $r+1$ is not a Laplacian eigenvalue. Now $\frac{r+2}{4}=m_1+1, \frac{3r}{2}=6m_1+3$ and $\frac{5r+2}{4}=5m_1+3$. Therefore, Theorem~\ref{Second theorem PGST} implies that the total graph of $Q_r$ exhibits Laplacian pretty good pair state transfer.
\end{ex}






















