where $\pi_2(\Lambda_\epsilon^k(W^{k,\H}),\Lambda_\epsilon^j(W^{j,\H}))$ (resp. $\pi_2(W^{k,\H},W^{j,\H})$) denotes the 2nd level of the rough path associated with  $(\Lambda_\epsilon^k(W^{k,\H}),\Lambda_\epsilon^j(W^{j,\H}))$ (resp. $(W^{k,\H},W^{j,\H})$), and $\delta_{1,2}=v_1\otimes v_2$, for $\{v_1,v_2\}$ canonical $\R^2$'s basis.

By trivial generalizations of equation \eqref{LambdaCont} and equation \eqref{LambdaUnifConv}, we have that $(\Lambda_\epsilon^k,\Lambda_\epsilon^j)$ is continuous and satisfies uniform convergence as $\epsilon$ vanishes. Therefore, \cite[Theorem 5]{friz2014convergence} implies that
\begin{equation}\label{i:Smallness2nd}
\begin{aligned}
