\section{Discretization}\label{sec:discrete}


Let the spatial domain $\ome$ be partitioned into a union of $M$ non-intersecting
elements $\elem$ forming a mesh $\grid = \cup_{i = 1}^M \elem_i$. Then we denote
by $\Gamma_i$ with unit normal $\mb{n}$ the set of all intersections between
two
elements of the grid $\grid$, and the set of all
intersections, also with the boundary of the domain $\Omega$, is denoted by
$\Gamma$.
For each element $\elem \in \grid$
we define the local mesh width as
\begin{equation}
\helem := \text{diam}(\elem) = \sup_{\mbx, \mby \in \elem} ||\mbx - \mby||.
\end{equation}
For an intersection $\isec \in \Gamma$ we denote the adjacent elements with $\elemin_\isec$ and
$\elemout_\isec$ ($\elemin_\isec = \elemout_\isec$ for $\isec \in \Gamma \cap \Gamma_i$) and
define
\begin{equation}
  h_\isec := \frac{\min\{|\elemin_\isec|,|\elemout_\isec|\}}{|\isec|} \quad \forall \isec \in \Gamma.
\end{equation}
The global mesh width is then defined as $h = \max_{\isec \in \Gamma} h_\isec$.
For our discretization methods we require, that the mesh has a certain
regularity.
\begin{remark}[Regularity]\label{rem:regularity}
If the mesh $\grid$ is a tessellation wherein all $K \in \grid$ are regular quadrilaterals or triangles then there exists a constant $C_{\grid} > 1$ only dependent on the shape of $K$ and dimension $d$ of $\Omega$ such that
\begin{equation}
\min\{h_{\elemout_\isec}, h_{\elemin_\isec}\} \leq C_\grid h_\isec \quad \forall \isec \in \Gamma.
\end{equation}
For instance when $\omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$, we have for  quadrilaterals a sharp constant $C_\grid = \sqrt{2}$, and for isosceles right triangles we have a sharp constant $C_\grid = 4$.
\end{remark}






