\par Now, we can construct, using the same procedure as for the cylinder, a $1-$form that lives outside the band that we just added. The remaining part is to see what to do inside the bands. First, note that the band should be treated as a part of the surface with $0$ area, as it is merely the thickening of a curve which of course has zero area. This leads to the natural condition of imposing that the holonomy around any closed curve inside the band is always equal to $1_G$. 
\par At first glance, we might be tempted to consider the following stronger condition 
\[A  \text{ restricted to the band } \equiv 0,\] 
which will turn out insufficient, as the measure we obtain with this condition is not quite the Yang--Mills measure. In fact, we can compute the joint law of the holonomies of the two outgoing component in this case. It is the solution at time $\pi$ to the following system of SDE
\[\begin{cases}
    \dd X_t = X_t \circ \dd \xi(\mathds{1}_{\square(0,t, 0, 1)}) \\
    \dd Y_t = Y_t \circ \dd \xi(\mathds{1}_{\square(\pi,\pi+t,0,1)})
\end{cases},\]
where we recall
\[\square(\theta_1,\theta_2,r_1,r_2) = \{(r,\theta) \in M\; \theta_1 \leq \theta \leq\theta_2; r_1\leq r\leq r_2\}.\]

However, the stochastic vector-valued process
\[\left(\xi(\mathds{1}_{\square(0,t, 0, 1)});\xi(\mathds{1}_{\square(\pi,\pi+t,0,1)}\right)_{0\leq t \leq \pi}\]
has the same law as
$(B^1_{F_g(t)}; B^2_{F_d(t)})$ where $B^1$ and $B^2$ are independent Brownian motions and 
\[F_1(t) = \sigma(\square(0,t, 0, 1)) \text{ and } F_2(t) = \sigma(\square(\pi,\pi+t, 0, 1)).\]
Therefore, 
\[  \mathbb{P}\left( X\in \dd x, Y\in \dd y\right) = p_{F_1(0.5)}(x)p_{F_2(0.5)}(y)\dd x\dd y,\]
where $(X,Y)$ is the random vector corresponding to the joint holonomies of the two outgoing boundary components of $\Sigma$.
Note that this is not what we would have obtained had we computed these holonomies using the Driver--Sengupta formula which yields 
\[  \mathbb{P}\left(X\in \dd x, Y\in \dd y\right) = p_{\sigma(M)}(xy) \dd x\dd y
 .\] 
\par Rather, the condition to consider is that $A$ within the band should be  gauge equivalent to the $0$ connection. Since we have already fixed the gauge, and we would like to conserve the fact that $\iota_{\nabla f} A \equiv 0$, the only freedom we have is to define $A$ inside the band as $c\dd\theta$. The most natural choice of $c$ due to gauge invariance is the $\log$ of a uniform random variable in $G$. Let us now calculate the new joint law of the holonomies of the outgoing boundary component. 

Denote by $H_1$ and $H_1$ the holonomies of the two outgoing boundary components of $\Sigma$. For a bounded measurable function $f:G\times G\rightarrow \R$,
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}[f(\mathrm{H}_1,\mathrm{H}_2)]=&\mathbb{E}[f(XU,YU^{-1})],
\end{align*}
where $X,Y$ are the solution to the same system as before, and $U$ is an independent uniform random variable in $G$, which represents the holonomy of each red curve in~\ref{fig:topview}. We get 
\begin{align*}
    \mathbb{E}[f(\mathrm{H}_1,\mathrm{H}_2)]=&\mathbb{E}[f(XU,YU^{-1})]=\int_{G^3}f(xu,yu^{-1})p_{\sigma_1}(x)p_{\sigma_1}(y)\dd x\dd y \dd u\\
    =&\int_{G^3}f(x,y)p_{\sigma_1}(xu^{-1})p_{\sigma_1}(yu)\dd x\dd y \dd u =\int_{G^2}f(x,y)p_{\sigma(M)}(xy)\dd x\dd y ,
\end{align*}
which now gives the correct density and gives positive arguments that the new construction yields the Yang--Mills measure. 
\paragraph{The case of a general surface. } \label{generalsurface}
