    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{TopVieSurface.png}
    \caption{Top view of a general surface with zero area bands.}
    \label{fig:SurfaceZeroArea}
\end{figure}

The case of the pant shaped surface is a good guide to the construction of the Yang--Mills measure in Morse gauge on a general surface. We already now that we need to have a resolution of the surface in such a way that given any point on the surface, there exists exactly one path that connects this point to the boundary of the surface. This will be made clear by the introduction of pseudo coordinates in Section~\ref{s:globalresolutionsurface}. These pseudo--coordinates will allow us to transfer the construction for the cylinder to all surfaces. The general formula is given in (\ref{YMFormula}). Let us here only calculate the law of the holonomy of the outgoing boundary component using this representation. 
\par Let $\Sigma$ be a surface with genus $2g$, one outgoing boundary component, and a smooth area form $\sigma$. In order to resolve the surface, we need to insert $2g$ bands of zero area. A top view can be seen in Figure~\ref{fig:SurfaceZeroArea}.
The holonomy of the boundary component is given by the product
\[\mathrm{Hol}(\partial \Sigma)=X_1U_1\cdots X_{2g}U_{2g}X_{2g+1}U_1^{-1}\cdots X_{2g}U_{2g}^{-1},\]
where $(U_i)_{1\leq i\leq 2g}$ are independent and Haar distributed $G-$valued random variables, and the $X_i$ are as before, the holonomy of the De Rham primitive of the white noise in the sector within $U_i, U_{i+1}$. 
Let $f$ be a measurable function. We have 
\begin{eqnarray*}
    \mathbb{E}[f(\mathrm{Hol}(\partial \Sigma))] 
    &=&\int_{G^{6g}} f(x_1u_1\cdots x_{2g}u_{2g}x_{2g+1}u_1^{-1}\cdots x_{4g}u_{2g}^{-1})p_{\sigma_1}(x_1)\cdots p_{\sigma_{4g}}(x_{4g})\dd x \dd u,
\end{eqnarray*}
where $\sigma_i$ is the are of the corresponding sector.
\begin{eqnarray*}
        \mathbb{E}[f(\mathrm{Hol}(\partial \Sigma))] &=&\int_{G^{6g}} f(\alpha)p_{\sigma_1}(\alpha u_{2g}x_{4g}^{-1}\cdots u_{1}x_{2g+1}^{-1} u_{2g}^{-1}x_{2g}^{-1}\cdots x_{2}^{-1} u_1^{-1})p_{\sigma_2}(x_2)\cdots p_{\sigma_{4g}}(x_{4g}) \dd x \dd u \\
    &=&\int_{G} f(\alpha) d\alpha \int_{G^{6g-1}}p_{\sigma(\Sigma)}(\alpha[a_1,b_1]\cdots [a_{2g},b_{2g}]) \dd a \dd b.
\end{eqnarray*}
Therefore, 
\[\mathbb{P}(\mathrm{Hol}(\partial \Sigma)\in \dd g) = \dd g \int_{G^{6g-1}}p_{\sigma(\Sigma)}(g[a_1,b_1]\cdots [a_{2g},b_{2g}]) \dd a \dd b,\]
which is the same as what we would have obtained from the Driver--Sengupta formula. 



