    The idea is to repeat the proof in~\ref{DriverSenForA} $4g$ times, in each of the portions of $\Sigma$ within two consecutive unstable curves. We get the global formula by independence of the white noise throughout these portions, followed by the insertion of the uniform random variables associated with the zero--area bands, as in~\ref{generalsurface}.

    We would like to relate our random connection with the one appearing in~\cite{BCDRT}.
   For any smooth oriented curve $\gamma$ everywhere transverse to the flow, we need to show that
    \begin{equation}\label{eq:integralAovergamma}
    \mathbf{W}_\gamma:= \int_\gamma \Psi^*\left(\tilde{A}d\theta \right)   
    \end{equation}
    is well--defined as a $\mathfrak{g}$-valued normal random variable with variance the area of a certain rectangle defined in terms of the curve $\gamma$.
    A key consequence from its definition is that $\Psi$ preserves the orientation.
    Beware that the curve $\gamma$ might intersect some unstable curve transversally hence its image $\Psi(\gamma)\subset \mathbf{Cyl}$ under $\Psi$ is only piecewise smooth~: $\Psi(\gamma)=\cup_{i=1}^k \gamma_i$ as a disjoint union of smooth curves. By transversality with the flow and since this transversality is preserved under mapping by $\Psi$, $\gamma_i\subset \mathbf{Cyl}$ is transverse with $\partial_r$ and  we can describe each curve $\gamma_i$ as some graph: $\theta\in I_i\subset [0,2\pi]\mapsto (\theta,r_i(\theta))$ where $r_i$ is a smooth function. A key consequence from its definition is that $\Psi$ preserves the orientation this is why we get the same orientation for all curves $\gamma_i$, they run counterclockwise if the initial curve $\gamma$ is oriented counterclockwise. Another consequence of transversality is that the open intervals
    $\mathrm{int}(I_i)_{i=1,\dots,k}$ are two by two disjoint.
     We decompose $\Psi_*[\gamma]=\sum_{i=1}^k [\gamma_i]$ where the r.h.s. is a finite sum of currents of integration of degree $1$ on the curves $\gamma_i, i=1,\dots,k$.
    The above integral in equation \ref{eq:integralAovergamma} can only defined probabilistically as follows~: 
\begin{align*}
\int_\gamma \Psi^*\left(\tilde{A}\dd\theta \right)=\left\langle \Psi_*[\gamma], \tilde{A}\dd\theta \right\rangle_{\mathbf{Cyl}}=\sum_{i=1}^k\xi( 1_{\square_i} )   
\end{align*}
where each domain $\square_i$ is defined as follows 
 $$\square_i:=\{ (r,\theta);0\leqslant r\leqslant r_i(\theta), \theta\in I_i  \}\subset \mathbf{Cyl} $$
 and each term $\xi( 1_{\square_i} ) $ is well--defined since each $1_{\square_i} \in L^2(\mathbf{Cyl}, \Psi_*\sigma)$ belongs to the Cameron Martin space of $\xi$.
Then from the above identities
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}\left( \int_\gamma \Psi^*\left(\tilde{A}\dd\theta \right)\otimes \int_\gamma \Psi^*\left(\tilde{A}\dd\theta \right) \right)=\mathbb{E}\left((\sum_{i=1}^k \xi(1_{\square_i}))\otimes(\sum_{i=1}^k \xi(1_{\square_i})) \right)=\sum_{i=1}^k \sigma(\square_i) \mathrm{Id}^{\mathfrak{g}\otimes \mathfrak{g}}  
\end{align*}
where we used the crucial fact that all mutual intersections $\square_i\cap \square_j, i\neq j$ have measure $0$. Now observe that $\sum_{i=1}^k \sigma(\square_i)$ is the area on $\mathcal{S}$ of the set $\{x\prec \gamma, x\in \mathcal{S}  \}:=\overline{\{\varphi_f^{-t}(x); x\in \gamma\}}$ which implies we recover the result
that $\mathbf{W}_\gamma$ has normal law with variance equals to the area $\sigma\left(\{x\prec \gamma  \} \right)$ which is one of the main result of \cite{BCDRT} on the Gaussian part of the free boundary Yang--Mills measure.
\end{proof}

We use the notion of strong solutions for Stratonovich SDE, when we reparametrize our driving Brownian motion by some $C^{1-}$ function, this notion of strong solution still exists and the area function is only $C^{1-}$ so there is no problem here. We refer the reader to a separate note for the detailed proof~\cite{NotesRDELiegroups} or the appendix of \cite{BCDRT}. 


\section{Morse lattice  from flowlines and level sets} \label{s:Morselattice}


We introduce a dyadic decomposition of our surface into rectangles whose sides are either flowlines of $\nabla f$ or level sets of $f$. The construction is simplified because of the existence of the global pseudo-coordinates $(r,\theta)$ on $\Sigma$.

There are $2g+2$ critical levels $c_1<\dots<c_{2g+2}$ of the Morse function $f$,
we decompose each piece $[c_{i},c_{i+1}]$ into $2^n$ levels of the form
\[c_i+\frac{k}{2^n}(c_{i+1}-c_i) , \,\,\,k\in \{0,\dots, 2^n\}.\]
Therefore we have $(2g+1)2^n$ level sets of the Morse function of the form 
\[f^{-1}( c_i+\frac{k}{2^n}(c_{i+1}-c_i)), \,\,\, i\in \{1,\dots,2g+1\} , k\in \{0,\dots,2^n\}.\] These levels are also mapped 
to a certain level decomposition of the cylinder \[[\min(f),\max(f)]\times \mathbb{S}^1\] under the map $\Psi$.
Recall we blow--up the surface $\Sigma$ at the minimum $a_1$, 
at the minimum $a_1$ 
the blown--up space becomes a circle parametrized by some angle $\theta\in [0,2\pi]$. 
We consider $0<\theta_1<\dots<\theta_{4g}<2\pi $ the $4g$ intersections of the  
stable curves of the flow $(\Phi^t)_{t\in \mathbb{R}}$ with the blow--up circle.
These corresponds to the intervals $(I_i)_{i=1}^{4g}$ as
\[I_1=[\theta_1,\theta_2], I_2=[\theta_2,\theta_3], \dots, I_{4g}=[\theta_{4g}, \theta_1].\]
Now we choose $2^n$ directions 
\[ \theta_i+\frac{k}{2^n}(\theta_{i+1}-\theta_i)), \,\,\,
i\in \{1,\dots, 4g-1\}, \, k\in \{0,\dots,2^n-1\},\] this decomposes each interval $I_i, i=1,\dots, 4g$ into $2^n$ dyadic subintervals.
Now we consider $2^n(4g)$ level sets of the global angular variable $\theta$, this yields a union of $2^n4g$  integral curves of $\Phi^{t},t\in \mathbb{R}$ which have these directions and intersect these $2^n4g$ curves with our $(2g+1)2^n$ levels of the Morse function $f$.
For every $n$, the above picture describes a sequence of graphs $(\Lambda^n)_n$ on our surface compatible with the Morse function and of dyadic resolution $2^{-n}$. 
Under the map $\Psi$, this is also canonically identified with a grid decomposition of 
\[\Psi(H_i)=[\min(f),\max(f)]\times I_i.\]
The grid is made of either flowlines of $\Phi^t$ or smooth arcs orthogonal to the flow and contained in levels of $f$.  
Our lattice gauge model will be defined on this Morse grid.  An illustrative example of a Morse grid is shown in~\ref{fig:MorseGrid}
